
const DAYS = [{"n":1,"kind":"learn","title":"What kind of variable is this","blurb":"Qualitative or quantitative, nominal or ordinal, discrete or continuous. Population against sample. The tricks behind the bread study","idea":"<p>Every variable gets two labels. The first is its <b>nature</b>: what kind of values it takes.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Qualitative</b>: names or categories. <i>Nominal</i> has no order (city, brand, blood type). <i>Ordinal</i> has an order but no fixed distances (economy &lt; business &lt; first; ratings 1 to 5).</li>\n<li><b>Quantitative</b>: numbers where arithmetic makes sense. <i>Discrete</i> is counted (children, complaints, bags). <i>Continuous</i> is measured (time, weight, price, temperature).</li></ul>\n<p>The test when digits appear: does an average make sense? Phone numbers, ID numbers and shirt numbers are labels, so they are nominal. A 1 to 5 rating is ordinal, even though it looks like a number.</p>\n<p>The second label is its <b>role</b>: the <i>dependent</i> variable is the one being explained (sales, salary); the <i>independent</i> one explains it (ad spend, education). Roles change with the question.</p>\n<p><b>Population</b> is everyone the question is about; the <b>sample</b> is who you measured. A number about the population is a <i>parameter</i>; the same number from the sample is a <i>statistic</i>.</p>\n<p>What each type allows: nominal, mode only. Ordinal, mode and median. Quantitative, mode, median and mean.</p>\n<p>The tricks the exam names: too small a sample, self-selection (people chose to answer), survivorship (you only see the winners), confirmation bias, base-rate neglect (\"98% of offenders eat bread\", but so does everyone), confounding (a third thing moves both), leading questions, misleading graphs, sponsored research.</p><p><b>Why anecdotes fail</b>, the slides' four reasons: too few observations, survivorship (you only hear about the winners), confirmation bias, and stories that mutate in the retelling.</p>","review":null,"qs":[{"q":"Which of these is quantitative and discrete?","o":["Number of product returns per week","Customer satisfaction on a 1 to 5 scale","Postal code of the customer","Time spent on the website","Payment method (card, cash, transfer)"],"a":0,"w":"Returns are counted: 0, 1, 2 and so on. The rating is ordinal, the postal code is a label, time is continuous, and payment method is nominal."},{"q":"A hotel chain codes its star rating as 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. What type of variable is it?","o":["Quantitative, continuous","Quantitative, discrete","Qualitative, ordinal","Qualitative, nominal","It depends on how many hotels there are"],"a":2,"w":"Stars are ordered categories. The gap from 4 to 5 stars is not the same \"amount\" as from 1 to 2. Coding them as numbers does not make them quantities."},{"q":"A bank has about 2 million branch visits a month. It measures the waiting time of 400 random visits and gets an average of 9.4 minutes. What is 9.4?","o":["A parameter","The population","A statistic","The unit of observation","The variable"],"a":2,"w":"9.4 comes from the sample, so it is a statistic. The parameter is the true average over all 2 million visits, which nobody observes directly."},{"q":"Ticket class (economy, business, first). Which measures of center are legal?","o":["Mode only","Mode and median","Mode, median and mean","Mean only","None of them"],"a":1,"w":"It is ordinal: there is an order, so a middle value (median) exists. There are no real distances, so you cannot average economy and first."},{"q":"\"98% of convicted offenders eat bread.\" What is the trick?","o":["Small sample","Leading question wording","Survivorship bias","Base-rate neglect","Misleading graph"],"a":3,"w":"Around 98% of everyone eats bread. Without comparing offenders to non-offenders, the number says nothing."},{"q":"A survey finds that firms using outside consultants have higher profits. Which statement is correct?","o":["Profit is the independent variable","Using consultants causes higher profits","Profit is dependent, consultant use (yes/no) is an independent qualitative variable, and a third factor such as firm size could drive both","Both variables are quantitative","Consultant use is dependent because it comes first"],"a":2,"w":"Profit is being explained, so it is dependent. Yes/no is qualitative. Bigger firms can afford consultants and also earn more, so the link is not proof of cause."}],"mistakes":["Calling anything written in digits \"quantitative\". Test it: does an average mean anything?","Calling what you measured \"the population\". The question defines the population, not convenience.","Taking an ordinal mean at face value. It can rise while the share of angry customers doubles."],"slides":[{"q":"The Lesson 1 questionnaire: IDF rank coded as a number (private = 1, corporal = 2, sergeant = 3). What type is it?","o":["Quantitative, discrete","Quantitative, continuous","Qualitative, ordered (ordinal)","Qualitative, nominal","It becomes quantitative once coded"],"a":2,"w":"Coding does not create quantity. \"Corporal minus private\" is not 1 of anything. Order exists, distances do not."},{"q":"Same questionnaire: calendar year. What type is it?","o":["Qualitative, nominal","Quantitative, discrete","Quantitative, continuous","Qualitative, ordinal","Not a variable"],"a":1,"w":"The slides count it as discrete: between 2024 and 2026 there is exactly one year."},{"q":"\"Tribes never exposed to bread show almost no Alzheimer's.\" What is the trick?","o":["Base-rate neglect","A leading question","Confounding: those are diseases of old age, and short life expectancy hides them","Too small a sample","A misleading graph"],"a":2,"w":"A third factor, how long people live, explains both."}]},{"n":2,"kind":"learn","title":"Tables and pictures","blurb":"Frequency, relative and cumulative columns. Class width and density. Which chart is legal, and how charts lie","idea":"<p>A frequency table counts how often each value appears. Three columns do the work:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">f(x) = count &nbsp;·&nbsp; p(x) = f(x)/n &nbsp;·&nbsp; F(x) = running total up to x</div>\n<p>The cumulative column only makes sense when the values have an order. \"Up to Facebook\" means nothing.</p>\n<p>Reading F: <b>\"at most x\"</b> is F(x). <b>\"At least x\"</b> is n minus F of the value just below x. \"Fewer than 2\" means at most 1, so F(1).</p>\n<p>Continuous data (or discrete with many values) is grouped into classes. Each class has a width ℓ and a <b>density</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">ℓ = upper − lower &nbsp;·&nbsp; d = f / ℓ</div>\n<p>In a histogram the bar height is d, not f. Then area = ℓ × d = f. When widths differ, a wide class looks big just because it is wide. The most crowded class is the one with the highest d.</p>\n<p>The cumulative graph (ogive) plots F at the upper bound of each class, joined by straight lines. It can never fall. Steep means crowded.</p>\n<p>Which chart: nominal or ordinal, bar (pie only for a few parts of a whole). Discrete, bar plus polygon. Continuous, histogram. Over time, line. Bars must start at zero; 3-D pies inflate the front slice.</p><p><b>Quick reads the slides drill</b>: \"shorter than 170\" is F up to 170. \"At least 180\" is n minus F up to 180. With equal widths, the largest f and the largest d always pick the same class.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. \"Number of employees in a firm\" is:","o":["Qualitative, nominal","Qualitative, ordinal","Quantitative, discrete","Quantitative, continuous","Not a variable"],"a":2,"w":"Headcount is counted, not measured."},"qs":[{"q":"Ten families have 2, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5, 5, 4, 3, 2 people. How many families have at least 3 people?","o":["5","3","6","7","2"],"a":3,"w":"F(2) = 3 families have 2 or fewer. At least 3 = n − F(2) = 10 − 3 = 7. (At most 3 would be F(3) = 5. Families of exactly 3 count in both.)"},{"q":"Gym members by age: 20–30: 10, 30–40: 20, 40–60: 10. In a correct histogram, what are the bar heights?","o":["10, 20, 10","1, 2, 0.5","1, 2, 1","0.25, 0.5, 0.25","10, 20, 20"],"a":1,"w":"Height is density: 10/10 = 1, 20/10 = 2, 10/20 = 0.5. The 40–60 class has the same count as 20–30 but is twice as wide, so it is half as tall. Both areas are 10."},{"q":"Which class is the most crowded?","t":"<table><tr><th>Class</th><td>0–10</td><td>10–15</td><td>15–30</td><td>30–40</td><td>40–60</td></tr><tr><th>f</th><td>20</td><td>15</td><td>30</td><td>18</td><td>24</td></tr></table>","o":["0–10","10–15","15–30","30–40","40–60"],"a":1,"w":"Densities: 20/10 = 2, 15/5 = 3, 30/15 = 2, 18/10 = 1.8, 24/20 = 1.2. The highest is 10–15, even though 15–30 has the most cases."},{"q":"You want to show a company's revenue for each of the last 24 months. Which chart?","o":["Pie chart","Histogram","Line chart","Cumulative graph","3-D bar chart"],"a":2,"w":"The x-axis is time. A line shows the trend, seasons and turning points."},{"q":"A slide shows \"Our brand 52%, rival 48%\" as bars on an axis from 47 to 53. Roughly how many times taller does our bar look?","o":["About 1.08 times","About 2 times","About 4 times","About 5 times","The same height"],"a":3,"w":"Only the part above 47 is drawn: 5 against 1. A real gap of 4 points looks like 5 to 1. Bars must start at zero."},{"q":"Forty exam scores give F = 5 at 60, 10 at 70, 20 at 75, 30 at 85, 40 at 100. What share scored 70 or more?","o":["25%","50%","75%","10%","30%"],"a":2,"w":"n − F(70) = 40 − 10 = 30 students, and 30/40 = 75%."}],"mistakes":["Drawing histogram heights as f or p when the class widths differ.","Answering \"at least x\" with n − F(x). Subtract F of the value one step below.","Adding a cumulative column to a nominal variable."],"slides":[{"q":"Manhattan, 24 October days: 5–8 °C: 4, 8–11: 8, 11–14: 6, 14–17: 5, 17–20: 1. On how many days was it at least 11 °C?","o":["6","18","12","14","10"],"a":2,"w":"n − F(up to 11) = 24 − 12 = 12."},{"q":"Heights of 72 students; F up to 180 cm is 50 and F up to 185 cm is 63. How many are at least 180 cm?","o":["9","22","50","63","13"],"a":1,"w":"72 − F(under 180) = 72 − 50 = 22. The slide itself catches the tempting 72 − 63."}]},{"n":3,"kind":"learn","title":"The center","blurb":"Mode, median, mean. Combined groups, centers from a table, the grouped median, and reading skew","idea":"<p>Three ways to say \"typical\":</p>\n<ul><li><b>Mode</b>: the most common value.</li><li><b>Median</b>: the middle of the sorted list, at position (n + 1)/2. With an even n, average the two middle values.</li><li><b>Mean</b>: sum divided by n.</li></ul>\n<p>The mean feels every value, so one extreme value pulls it hard. The median only cares about order, so it barely moves. That is why salaries and house prices are reported as medians.</p>\n<p><b>Combining groups</b>: weight each group mean by its size. 30 calls at 6 minutes and 20 at 11 give (30·6 + 20·11)/50 = 8, not 8.5.</p>\n<p><b>From a table</b>: mean = Σx·f / n. Median: find where the cumulative column passes n/2.</p>\n<p><b>Grouped median</b>: find the class where F first reaches n/2, then walk into it:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">Median = L + ((n/2 − F before) / f) × width</div>\n<p>Do not just take the class midpoint.</p>\n<p><b>Skew</b>: the mean is pulled toward the tail. Mean above median means a right tail; mean below median means a left tail. Under skew, quote the median as \"typical\".</p><p><b>The Σ rules</b>: Σ respects plus, minus and constants: Σa = n·a, Σa·x = a·Σx, Σ(x + y) = Σx + Σy. It does not respect products or powers: Σxy ≠ Σx·Σy and Σx² ≠ (Σx)². Expand first, then sum.</p>\n<p><b>Grouped mode</b>: the modal class is the one with the highest <i>density</i>; report its midpoint.</p>\n<p><b>Shape order</b>: right skew, mode &lt; median &lt; mean. Left skew, mean &lt; median &lt; mode. So under right skew, <i>more than half</i> the values sit below the mean. The slides use this as a true/false exam question.</p>\n<p><b>Two more averages</b> from the Lesson 4 warm-up. Average speed over equal distances is the harmonic mean: 100 km/h there and 50 back is 66.7 km/h, not 75. Growth rates compound, so use the geometric mean: +100%, −50%, −50% averages −20.6% a day, not 0%.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. Classes 0–4 (f = 8) and 4–5 (f = 6). Which is more crowded?","o":["0–4, it has more cases","4–5, its density is 6 against 2","They are equal","Cannot tell without n","0–4, it is wider"],"a":1,"w":"d = 8/4 = 2 and 6/1 = 6. Density, not count."},"qs":[{"q":"Eight monthly salaries (NIS): 9,000; 9,500; 10,000; 10,500; 11,000; 11,500; 12,000; 58,000. What is the median?","o":["10,500","11,000","16,437.5","10,750","There is no median"],"a":3,"w":"n = 8, so average the 4th and 5th values: (10,500 + 11,000)/2 = 10,750. The mean is 16,437.5, pulled up by the founder."},{"q":"40 customers spent 150 NIS on average and 60 customers spent 200 NIS on average. What is the overall mean?","o":["175","180","185","190","200"],"a":1,"w":"(40·150 + 60·200)/100 = 18,000/100 = 180. The simple average, 175, ignores that the second group is bigger."},{"q":"Complaints per day over 40 days: 0 → 4 days, 1 → 9, 2 → 12, 3 → 8, 4 → 5, 5 → 2. What is the mean?","o":["2.5","2","2.175","1.45","3"],"a":2,"w":"Σx·f = 0 + 9 + 24 + 24 + 20 + 10 = 87. 87/40 = 2.175. A fraction is fine for a count."},{"q":"A firm reports salary mean 61 and median 48 (thousand NIS). Which is right?","o":["Symmetric, quote either","Left-skewed, quote the mean","Right-skewed, the typical salary is closer to 48","Right-skewed, quote 61 as typical","Left-skewed, quote the median"],"a":2,"w":"Mean above median means a long right tail of high salaries. The median speaks for the typical employee."},{"q":"Monthly spend of 200 customers. Estimate the median.","t":"<table><tr><th>NIS</th><td>0–100</td><td>100–200</td><td>200–300</td><td>300–400</td><td>400–500</td></tr><tr><th>f</th><td>30</td><td>60</td><td>50</td><td>45</td><td>15</td></tr></table>","o":["250","220","200","227.5","240"],"a":1,"w":"F = 30, 90, 140. n/2 = 100 is first reached in 200–300 (F before = 90, f = 50). Median = 200 + (100 − 90)/50 × 100 = 220. 250 is the midpoint trap; 227.5 is the mean."},{"q":"Course grade: homework 10%, participation 5%, quizzes 5%, final exam 80%. A student has 88, 100 and 90 in the first three. What exam grade gets a final of at least 80?","o":["80","75","About 77.1","About 72.1","About 76.3"],"a":2,"w":"Non-exam points: 8.8 + 5 + 4.5 = 18.3. Need 0.8·x ≥ 61.7, so x ≥ 77.125."}],"mistakes":["Averaging group means without weighting by group size.","Taking the class midpoint as the grouped median.","Treating a correct extreme value as an error. Keep it; choose the median."],"slides":[{"q":"x = 1, 2, 3 and y = 3, 4, 6. What is Σ(x + y)²?","o":["75","361","133","78","19"],"a":2,"w":"Expand: Σx² + 2Σxy + Σy² = 14 + 58 + 61 = 133. 75 drops the cross-term; 361 squares the sum."},{"q":"Scores in classes 40–60 (5), 60–70 (5), 70–75 (10), 75–85 (10), 85–100 (10). What is the mode?","o":["80","72.5","92.5","75.6","There is no mode"],"a":1,"w":"Three classes tie on f = 10. Density breaks the tie: 70–75 has d = 2. Its midpoint is 72.5."},{"q":"\"Graduate wages are right-skewed, so more graduates earn ABOVE the average wage than below it.\" True?","o":["True, the tail pulls people up","False: the mean sits above the median, so more than half earn below the mean","True, because mean = median","False, because the median is above the mean","Cannot tell without data"],"a":1,"w":"Right skew drags the mean past the halfway point."},{"q":"You drive 100 km at 100 km/h and back at 50 km/h. Average speed for the trip?","o":["75 km/h","70.7 km/h","66.7 km/h","50 km/h","80 km/h"],"a":2,"w":"200 km in 3 hours. The harmonic mean is the honest average over equal distances."}]},{"n":4,"kind":"learn","title":"The spread","blurb":"Quartiles, IQR and range. Variance and standard deviation, divided by n. What adding and multiplying do","idea":"<p><b>Quartiles</b>: sort the data. Q1 is at position (n + 1)/4, the median at (n + 1)/2, Q3 at 3(n + 1)/4.</p>\n<div class=\"f\">Range = max − min &nbsp;·&nbsp; IQR = Q3 − Q1</div>\n<p>The IQR covers the middle half and ignores extremes. The range is hostage to its two most extreme values.</p>\n<p><b>Variance</b> is the average squared distance from the mean. This course always divides by n (Excel VAR.P, STDEV.P):</p>\n<div class=\"f\">s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / n = Σx²/n − x̄² &nbsp;·&nbsp; s = √s²</div>\n<p>For grouped data use class midpoints, weighted by f. Any percentile works like the grouped median: L + ((p·n − F before)/f) × width.</p>\n<p><b>Changing the data</b>, y = a + b·x:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">mean → a + b·mean &nbsp;·&nbsp; s → |b|·s &nbsp;·&nbsp; s² → b²·s²</div>\n<p>Adding a constant moves everything and changes no spread. Multiplying stretches every spread by |b|, and the variance by b².</p>\n<p>Reading a five-number summary: if the median sits closer to Q1, the middle half is right-skewed. A maximum far beyond Q3 points to a high tail.</p><p><b>Percentiles on a frequency table</b>, the slides' \"dial\": dial = n × z / 100, walk down F, and the first value whose F reaches the dial is the answer. Q1 = P25, median = P50, Q3 = P75, first decile = P10. For grouped data the slides name the class; the assignments go further and interpolate with L + ((p·n − F)/f) × width. Know both.</p>\n<p><b>Skew from quartiles</b>: symmetric if Q3 − Q2 = Q2 − Q1. Right skew stretches the upper gap; left skew the lower one. This detector ignores outliers.</p>\n<p><b>A riddle the slides love</b>: range 0 forces IQR 0 (every value is the same). But IQR 0 says nothing about the range.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. Mean 52, median 44. Where is the tail?","o":["Left","Right","No tail","Both sides","Cannot tell"],"a":1,"w":"The mean is pulled toward the tail. Mean above median, tail on the right."},"qs":[{"q":"Delivery times (days): 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12. What is the IQR?","o":["10","4","5","3","2"],"a":1,"w":"n = 11. Positions 3 and 9: Q1 = 3, Q3 = 7, IQR = 4. The range is 10."},{"q":"Data: 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9. What is the standard deviation, as this course computes it?","o":["2.14","4","2","4.57","1.5"],"a":2,"w":"Mean = 40/8 = 5. Squared deviations sum to 9+1+1+1+0+0+4+16 = 32. s² = 32/8 = 4, s = 2. Dividing by 7 gives 2.14, the textbook version."},{"q":"Salaries have mean 12,000 and s = 1,500. Everyone gets a 6% raise. What is the new variance?","o":["2,250,000","2,385,000","2,528,100","1,590","2,340,900"],"a":2,"w":"s becomes 1.06 × 1,500 = 1,590. The variance is s², so it grows by 1.06²: 1,590² = 2,528,100."},{"q":"Same firm. Instead, everyone gets a flat 800 NIS raise. What is the new standard deviation?","o":["2,300","1,500","1,590","800","1,580"],"a":1,"w":"Adding a constant moves the mean to 12,800 and leaves every distance between people the same. s stays 1,500."},{"q":"Using the 200-customer spend table (0–100: 30, 100–200: 60, 200–300: 50, 300–400: 45, 400–500: 15), find the 90th percentile.","o":["About 389","About 400","About 350","About 445","About 322"],"a":0,"w":"p·n = 180. F = 30, 90, 140, 185: first reached in 300–400 (F before = 140, f = 45). 300 + (180 − 140)/45 × 100 = 388.9. 322 is Q3."},{"q":"Five-number summary: min 20, Q1 40, median 45, Q3 70, max 75. Which is correct?","o":["IQR is 55","The distribution is symmetric","IQR is 30 and the middle half is right-skewed, because the median sits much closer to Q1","Half the values exceed 70","The mean is 45"],"a":2,"w":"IQR = 70 − 40 = 30 (55 is the range). The median is 5 above Q1 and 25 below Q3. Only a quarter of values exceed Q3, and the mean cannot be read from quartiles."}],"mistakes":["Using n instead of n + 1 for quartile positions, or reading them from unsorted data.","Dividing by n − 1. This course divides by n.","Forgetting to square the factor for the variance.","Letting an added constant change the standard deviation."],"slides":[{"q":"Daily coffee, 200 patients: x = 0..7 with f = 10, 16, 12, 27, 55, 48, 15, 17. What is P80?","o":["4 cups","5 cups","6 cups","160","7 cups"],"a":1,"w":"Dial = 160. F = 10, 26, 38, 65, 120, 168: first to reach 160 is x = 5. 160 is the seat count, not the answer."},{"q":"Same coffee data. What is the IQR?","o":["7 cups","1 cup","2 cups","3 cups","100"],"a":2,"w":"Q1: dial 50 → 3 cups. Q3: dial 150 → 5 cups. IQR = 2."},{"q":"A dataset has an interquartile range of exactly 0. What is its range?","o":["Also 0","It can be any value of 0 or more","Exactly the median","Undefined","Twice the IQR"],"a":1,"w":"Only the middle half is forced to one value. The tails are free."},{"q":"Grades 89, 71, 88, 83, 61, 77, 91. What is the variance?","o":["121","103.71","10.18","121.00, dividing by 6","80"],"a":1,"w":"Mean 80. Σd² = 726. 726/7 = 103.71; s = 10.18. The 61 alone contributes 361."}]},{"n":5,"kind":"learn","title":"Where do you stand","blurb":"z-scores both ways. The coefficient of variation. The bell and Chebyshev","idea":"<p>A <b>z-score</b> says how many standard deviations a value sits above or below its own mean:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">z = (x − x̄) / s &nbsp;·&nbsp; back again: x = x̄ + z·s</div>\n<p>Use it to compare results from different scales. Always standardize against the person's own group. Standardized data always have mean 0 and s = 1. Adding a bonus to everyone does not change anyone's z.</p>\n<p>The <b>coefficient of variation</b> compares spread across different units:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">CV = s / x̄</div>\n<p>It fails when the mean is near zero, or the zero is arbitrary (°C, rating scales).</p>\n<p><b>If the data are bell-shaped</b>: about 68% within 1 s of the mean, 95% within 2 s (1.960 exactly), 99.7% within 3 s. 90% lie within 1.645 s, and the top 5% start at x̄ + 1.645·s.</p>\n<p><b>For any shape (Chebyshev)</b>: at least 1 − 1/k² lie within k standard deviations. At least 75% within 2, at least 88.9% within 3. It is a statement about the share of the data, not about any single unit.</p><p><b>Reading |z|</b>, the slides' ladder: under 1, the crowd. 1 to 2, notable. 2 to 3, rare. Over 3, alarm: talent, a data error, or a different population.</p>\n<p><b>Chebyshev, the other way round</b>: at most 1/z² of any data lie beyond ±z. Beyond 2: at most 25%. Beyond 3: at most 11%. Beyond 4: at most 6%.</p>\n<p><b>Two slide traps</b>. In a left-skewed distribution the fifth decile (the median) sits above the mean, so its z is <i>positive</i>. And a 7% raise for everyone leaves everyone's z unchanged: the salary, the mean and s all grow by 1.07, and it cancels.</p>\n<p><b>Percentile or z?</b> A percentile is your rank: robust, capped at 100, blind to how far ahead you are. z is your distance: it hears magnitude and has no cap, but outliers pull it. IQ is z in disguise: IQ = 100 + 15z.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. Scores have s = 6. The lecturer adds 5 points to everyone. New s?","o":["11","6","30","1.2","36"],"a":1,"w":"Adding a constant never changes spread."},"qs":[{"q":"Maya scored 82 on test A (mean 70, s 8). Tom scored 88 on test B (mean 80, s 10). Who did better against their own pool?","o":["Tom, 88 is higher","Maya, z = 1.5 against 0.8","Tom, z = 1.5 against 0.8","Equal, both are above the mean","They cannot be compared"],"a":1,"w":"Maya: (82 − 70)/8 = 1.5. Tom: (88 − 80)/10 = 0.8. Raw scores from different scales cannot be compared."},{"q":"A quiz has mean 40 and s 6. What raw score has z = −2?","o":["28","52","38","34","46"],"a":0,"w":"x = 40 + (−2)·6 = 28."},{"q":"Sales: mean 16,000 NIS, s 3,200. Lead time: mean 12 days, s 1.8. Which is relatively more variable?","o":["Lead time, CV 15%","Sales, CV 20% against 15%","Sales, because 3,200 > 1.8","They are equally variable","CV cannot compare different units"],"a":1,"w":"CV = 3,200/16,000 = 20% and 1.8/12 = 15%. Comparing 3,200 shekels with 1.8 days directly is meaningless; dividing by the mean removes the units."},{"q":"Bottles: mean 500 ml, s 4 ml, shape unknown. At least what share lie in 488 to 512 ml?","o":["75%","95%","88.9%","99.7%","68%"],"a":2,"w":"488 to 512 is 500 ± 3·4, so k = 3. Chebyshev: at least 1 − 1/9 = 88.9%. 99.7% would need a bell shape."},{"q":"Now the fills are shown to be bell-shaped. Above what fill level are the top 5% of bottles?","o":["508 ml","506.6 ml","504 ml","507.8 ml","512 ml"],"a":1,"w":"Top 5% start at x̄ + 1.645·s = 500 + 1.645·4 = 506.58."},{"q":"The teacher adds 5 points to every student. A student had z = +1.5 before. After?","o":["+1.5","+2.33","+2.0","+1.0","It depends on the new mean"],"a":0,"w":"The mean rises by 5 and s is unchanged, so the distance in standard deviations is the same."}],"mistakes":["Comparing raw scores across tests with different means and spreads.","Using the CV for temperatures, ratings, or a mean near zero.","Using 68-95-99.7 when nobody showed the data are bell-shaped."],"slides":[{"q":"A class: height mean 174 cm, s 14. Weight mean 60 kg, s 9. Which is more spread out, relative to its mean?","o":["Height, because 14 > 9","Weight: CV 15% against 8%","They are equal","You cannot compare cm and kg","Height: CV 15% against 8%"],"a":1,"w":"CV = 14/174 ≈ 8% and 9/60 = 15%. The raw comparison points the wrong way."},{"q":"\"In a left-skewed distribution, the standard score of the fifth decile is negative.\" True?","o":["True","False: the fifth decile is the median, which sits above the mean here, so its z is positive","False: it is always 0","True, because the tail is negative","Cannot tell"],"a":1,"w":"Left skew: mean < median, so (median − mean)/s > 0."},{"q":"Salaries: mean 5,700, s 700. Daniela has z = 2.5. Everyone gets a 7% raise. Her new z?","o":["2.675","2.5","2.34","3.5","It depends on her salary"],"a":1,"w":"Her salary, the mean and s all multiply by 1.07. The factors cancel."},{"q":"You got 85 in Statistics (mean 82, s 5) and 80 in Micro (mean 70, s 3). Which is stronger?","o":["Statistics, 85 is higher","Micro: z 3.33 against 0.60","Statistics: z 3.33 against 0.60","Equal","Cannot compare"],"a":1,"w":"(85 − 82)/5 = 0.60. (80 − 70)/3 = 3.33."},{"q":"Distribution shape unknown. At most what share of values lie more than 3 standard deviations from the mean?","o":["0.3%","5%","About 11%","25%","33%"],"a":2,"w":"1/3² = 1/9 ≈ 11%. The 0.3% figure only holds for a bell shape."}]},{"n":6,"kind":"learn","title":"The verdict","blurb":"Hypotheses, the t-table, one and two samples, and the two kinds of error","idea":"<p>Four steps, always in this order:</p>\n<ol><li><b>H0</b> is the presumption, with \"=\". <b>H1</b> is the claim: ≠, &gt; or &lt;. The direction is fixed before seeing data.</li><li>Choose α.</li><li>Compute t.</li><li>Compare with the table and write the verdict.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"f\">One sample: t = (x̄ − μ₀) / (s/√n), df = n − 1</div>\n<div class=\"f\">Two samples: t = (x̄₁ − x̄₂) / √(s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂), df = n₁ + n₂ − 2</div>\n<p><b>Reading the table</b>: row = df. A one-direction claim (&gt; or &lt;) reads the top header (one-tail). \"Differs\" (≠) reads the bottom header (two-tail). If df is not printed, use the nearest smaller row.</p>\n<p><b>Deciding</b>: two-sided, reject if |t| &gt; t*. One-sided, reject only if t is beyond t* in the claimed direction (below −t* for &lt;).</p>\n<p><b>Wording</b>: \"At the α level there is (not) enough evidence to reject H0.\" Never \"accept H0\".</p>\n<p><b>Errors</b>: Type I is rejecting a true H0; its chance is α. Type II is missing a false H0 (β). With a fixed sample, lowering α raises β. Only a bigger sample lowers both.</p><p><b>The verdict matrix</b>: power = 1 − β, the chance of catching a real effect. Choosing α is choosing whose mistake you can live with: drug approval fears Type I (tiny α), airport security fears Type II (loose α), spam filters fear Type I (burying a real email).</p>\n<p><b>The p-value</b>: the smallest α at which you would reject. Reject if p &lt; α. Same verdict as the table.</p>\n<p><b>When the t-test is legal</b>: a random sample, and a roughly bell-shaped population or n of 30 or more.</p>\n<p class=\"warn\"><b>One conflict in your files.</b> One version of the Lesson 6 slides (the recorded one) uses a \"conservative\" two-sample df: the smaller of n₁ − 1 and n₂ − 1. The other version, assignment 6A and both practice exams use df = n₁ + n₂ − 2, and the practice exams mark \"smaller n minus 1\" as a wrong answer. Use n₁ + n₂ − 2. If an exam question states its own df rule, follow the question.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. Quiz mean 40, s 6. What z does a score of 31 have?","o":["−1.5","−9","1.5","−0.67","−2"],"a":0,"w":"(31 − 40)/6 = −1.5."},"qs":[{"q":"One sample, n = 13, H1: μ ≠ μ₀, α = 0.05. What is t*?","o":["2.160","1.782","2.179","2.201","1.771"],"a":2,"w":"df = 12. Two-sided, so the bottom header 0.05: 2.179. 1.782 is the one-tail number."},{"q":"Two samples, 25 and 25, H1: μ₁ ≠ μ₂, α = 0.05. Which row and value?","o":["Row 24, 2.064","Row 48, not printed, so row 60: 2.000","Row 48, not printed, so row 40: 2.021","Row 50, 2.009","Row 25, 2.060"],"a":2,"w":"df = 25 + 25 − 2 = 48. Not printed, so take the nearest smaller row, 40: 2.021."},{"q":"An airline claims mean delay 12 minutes. Sample of 25: x̄ = 14.1, s = 6. The group claims the airline understates delays. Which is right?","o":["t = 0.35, do not reject","t = 1.75: reject at 5% one-sided (1.711), but a two-sided test at 5% (2.064) would not reject","t = 1.75: reject at 5% one-sided and two-sided","t = 1.75: do not reject at 5% one-sided","t = 8.75, reject"],"a":1,"w":"SE = 6/√25 = 1.2. t = 2.1/1.2 = 1.75, df 24. It clears the one-tail bar 1.711 but not the two-tail bar 2.064. The direction had to be chosen before the data."},{"q":"H1: μ < 5, t = −2.55, df = 7, α = 0.01. Verdict?","o":["Reject, because −2.55 < 2.998","Do not reject: −2.55 is not below −2.998","Reject, because |−2.55| > 1.895","Do not reject, because t is negative","Reject at every level"],"a":1,"w":"Lower-tail test: the rejection zone is below −2.998 (row 7, one-tail 0.01). −2.55 is not that far. At 5% (−1.895) it would reject."},{"q":"A/B test. A: n = 10, x̄ = 212, s = 40. B: n = 12, x̄ = 248, s = 48. The team claimed in advance that B raises order value. Which is right?","o":["t ≈ 1.92, df 20: reject one-sided at 5% (1.725), not two-sided at 5% (2.086)","t ≈ 1.92, df 11: do not reject","t ≈ 0.85, do not reject","t ≈ 1.92, df 20: reject two-sided at 5%","t ≈ 1.92, df 22: reject at 1%"],"a":0,"w":"SE = √(1,600/10 + 2,304/12) = √352 ≈ 18.76. t = 36/18.76 ≈ 1.92. df = 20. The declared one-sided claim passes at 5%."},{"q":"In a court, H0 is \"innocent\". What is a Type I error?","o":["Letting a guilty person go","Convicting an innocent person","Not holding a trial","Choosing α too large","Using a two-sided test"],"a":1,"w":"Type I = rejecting a true H0. Letting the guilty go is Type II."}],"mistakes":["Reading the two-tail header for a one-sided claim, or the reverse.","Forgetting √n: the denominator is s/√n, not s.","Two samples: df is n₁ + n₂ − 2.","Writing \"H0 is true\" or \"we accept H0\".","Choosing the direction of H1 after seeing the data."],"slides":[{"q":"A coffee chain promises a 4-minute average wait. n = 16, x̄ = 4.6, s = 1.2. No direction was stated. Verdict at 5%?","o":["t = 2.00, reject","t = 2.00, df 15, two-sided bar 2.131: do not reject","t = 0.5, do not reject","t = 2.00, df 16, reject","t = 8.0, reject"],"a":1,"w":"SE = 1.2/4 = 0.3. t = 0.6/0.3 = 2.00. Two-sided 5%, row 15: 2.131. It would reject one-sided (1.753), or two-sided at 10%."},{"q":"A laptop maker claims 10 hours of battery. Testers suspect LESS. n = 9, x̄ = 9.4, s = 0.9. At 5%?","o":["t = −2.00, df 8: reject (bar −1.860)","t = −2.00: do not reject","t = −0.67: do not reject","t = −2.00, reject at 1% too","t = −6.00: reject"],"a":0,"w":"SE = 0.9/3 = 0.3. t = −2.00. One-tail 5%, row 8: 1.860. At 1% (2.896) it would not reject."},{"q":"A spam filter: H0 = \"the email is legitimate\". Which error should its designers fear more?","o":["Type II: junk reaching the inbox","Type I: a real email, maybe a job offer, buried in spam","Neither, they are equal","Type I, because α is always 5%","Type II, because β is unknown"],"a":1,"w":"The slides: filters are kept deliberately lenient."},{"q":"\"Families here earn less than $1 a day.\" n = 25, x̄ = 0.92, s = 0.25. At 5%?","o":["Reject: the mean is below 1","t = −1.60, df 24: not beyond −1.711, do not reject","t = −1.60: reject","t = −0.32: do not reject","t = −1.60, reject at 5% two-sided"],"a":1,"w":"SE = 0.25/5 = 0.05. t = −0.08/0.05 = −1.60. The sample leans the claim's way but does not clear the bar. At 10% (−1.318) it would."}]},{"n":7,"kind":"learn","title":"Two variables together","blurb":"Scatter plots, covariance, Pearson r and its ladder. The portfolio law","idea":"<p>Look at the scatter plot before any number. Only the eye sees curves, clusters and outliers.</p>\n<div class=\"f\">cov(x, y) = Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ) / n</div>\n<p>Only its sign means anything: together (+), opposite (−), no straight-line link (0). Its size carries units like \"shekels times days\", so do not read it.</p>\n<div class=\"f\">r = cov / (sₓ · sᵧ), always between −1 and +1</div>\n<p>r has no units. Change NIS to thousands and cov changes, r does not.</p>\n<p><b>The ladder for |r|</b>: 0 to 0.10 negligible, 0.10 to 0.39 weak, 0.40 to 0.69 medium, 0.70 to 0.89 strong, 0.90 to 1 very strong.</p>\n<p><b>Three guard rails</b>: r only sees straight lines (a perfect U-curve can give 0). One wild point can create or destroy r. Moving together is not causing.</p>\n<p><b>The portfolio law</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"f\">var = w²·var(x) + (1 − w)²·var(y) + 2·w·(1 − w)·cov</div>\n<p>A negative covariance subtracts risk. That is diversification.</p><p><b>r ignores steepness</b> (from Lesson 7): every perfect line scores ±1, steep or shallow. How much y moves per step is the slope's job, not r's. A perfectly flat line has no r at all, because y never varies.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. Two samples of 12 and 15. What df?","o":["25","27","11","14","26"],"a":0,"w":"n₁ + n₂ − 2 = 25."},"qs":[{"q":"Ad spend x = 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and sales y = 5, 4, 8, 7, 11. What is cov(x, y)?","o":["30","7.5","6","0.87","40"],"a":2,"w":"x̄ = 6, ȳ = 7. Products of deviations: 8, 6, 0, 0, 16. Sum 30, divided by 5 = 6."},{"q":"Same data: sₓ = √8, sᵧ = √6. What is r, and where is it on the ladder?","o":["0.75, strong","0.87, strong","0.87, very strong","6, very strong","0.5, medium"],"a":1,"w":"r = 6/√48 ≈ 0.87. That is in 0.70 to 0.89: strong."},{"q":"Finance re-enters sales in NIS instead of thousands. What happens?","o":["cov and r both multiply by 1,000","cov multiplies by 1,000, r stays 0.87","Both unchanged","r multiplies by 1,000","cov unchanged, r changes"],"a":1,"w":"Covariance carries units. r divides them out."},{"q":"In a drug study r = 0.02 between dose and benefit. What must be checked before saying \"no relationship\"?","o":["The sample mean","The scatter plot: benefit may rise and then fall","The t-table","The covariance, which must be large","Nothing, r settles it"],"a":1,"w":"A peak-shaped curve gives r near 0. r = 0 means no straight-line link, not no link."},{"q":"Two stocks: var 32 and 10.4, covariance −14.4. Half in each. What is the portfolio standard deviation?","o":["4.44%","1.84%","3.26%","4.22%","3.4%"],"a":1,"w":"0.25·32 + 0.25·10.4 + 0.5·(−14.4) = 8 + 2.6 − 7.2 = 3.4. √3.4 ≈ 1.84%. The average of the two risks is 4.44%."},{"q":"r = −0.45 between price and units sold. Ladder verdict?","o":["Weak, negative","Medium, negative","Strong, negative","Medium, positive","Negligible"],"a":1,"w":"|r| = 0.45 is in 0.40 to 0.69: medium. The sign says higher prices go with fewer units."}],"mistakes":["Computing before drawing.","Reading the size of a covariance.","Skipping zero-product rows. They add 0 but still count in n.","Forgetting the squares on the weights, or the 2 in front of cov.","Turning \"moves together\" into \"causes\"."],"slides":[{"q":"Hours studied 1–5 and grades 60, 65, 75, 70, 80. What are cov and r?","o":["cov 45, r 0.90","cov 9, r 0.90","cov 9, r 0.81","cov 0.9, r 9","cov 9, r 1.0"],"a":1,"w":"Σdₓdᵧ = 45, /5 = 9. sₓ = √2, sᵧ = √50. r = 9/10 = 0.90."},{"q":"War stock C (σ 31%) and peace stock D (σ 82%), cov −0.103. A 50/50 mix has risk of about:","o":["56.5%","37.5%","13.5%","82%","0%"],"a":1,"w":"0.25·0.094 + 0.25·0.675 − 0.5·0.103 ≈ 0.141; √0.141 ≈ 37.5%. The average of the two risks is 56.5%."}]},{"n":8,"kind":"learn","title":"The line","blurb":"Least squares by hand, residuals and R², reading a line, and the software printout","idea":"<div class=\"f\">ŷ = β̂₀ + β̂₁·x &nbsp;·&nbsp; β̂₁ = Σdₓ·dᵧ / Σdₓ² &nbsp;·&nbsp; β̂₀ = ȳ − β̂₁·x̄</div>\n<p>The slope is the covariance divided by the variance of x. The line always passes through (x̄, ȳ).</p>\n<p><b>Residual</b> e = y − ŷ: data minus model. Least squares picks the line with the smallest Σe². Residuals sum to zero.</p>\n<div class=\"f\">R² = 1 − Σe² / Σdᵧ² = r² &nbsp;(in simple regression)</div>\n<p>R² is the share of the variation in y the line explains. It is never negative. To go back: r = ±√R², with the slope's sign.</p>\n<p><b>Reading</b>: slope, \"per one extra unit of x, ŷ changes by β̂₁ on average\". Intercept, ŷ at x = 0, meaningful only if x = 0 is in the data. Predicting outside the data range is extrapolation. A line shows moving together, not cause.</p>\n<p><b>Printouts</b>: t = (coefficient − value tested) / SE, df = n − 2. The printed t tests \"= 0\". To test β = 1, compute (β̂ − 1)/SE yourself. CAPM: β is the slope (market sensitivity; above 1 is aggressive), α is the intercept (return the market does not explain).</p><p><b>Two more ways to write the slope</b>: β̂₁ = cov / sₓ² = r · sᵧ / sₓ. So the slope always has r's sign, and r = 0 means a flat line.</p>\n<p><b>Data = model + error</b>: yᵢ = β₀ + β₁xᵢ + εᵢ. Greek letters are the population truth; hats are estimates. Regressing y on x and x on y gives two different lines: the direction is your choice, not the data's.</p>\n<p><b>R² in the wild</b>: 0.10 can be a real finding in messy human data; 0.99 usually means a textbook, or a mistake.</p>","review":{"q":"Quick look back. r = −0.8. What is true?","o":["The line slopes down","The link is weak","cov is positive","r² is −0.64","Nothing about direction"],"a":0,"w":"The sign of r is the sign of the slope."},"qs":[{"q":"Using x̄ = 6, ȳ = 7, Σdₓdᵧ = 30, Σdₓ² = 40, what is the fitted line?","o":["ŷ = 7 + 0.75x","ŷ = 2.5 + 0.75x","ŷ = 1.5 + 0.75x","ŷ = 2.5 + 1.33x","ŷ = 6 + 0.87x"],"a":1,"w":"Slope 30/40 = 0.75. Intercept 7 − 0.75·6 = 2.5."},{"q":"Rent = 1,200 + 45·size (m²), fitted on 40 to 120 m². An 80 m² flat rents for 5,400. What is its residual?","o":["−600","+600","4,800","+1,200","0"],"a":1,"w":"Predicted 1,200 + 45·80 = 4,800. e = 5,400 − 4,800 = +600: it rents for 600 more than size alone predicts."},{"q":"Same model, R² = 0.64. What is r?","o":["0.64","0.41","+0.8","−0.8","0.36"],"a":2,"w":"√0.64 = 0.8, positive because the slope is positive."},{"q":"A client asks for the predicted rent of a 300 m² penthouse from the same model. Best answer?","o":["14,700 NIS","It is an extrapolation far beyond 40 to 120 m²; the number has no support in the data","1,200 NIS","13,500 NIS","Use R² to adjust it"],"a":1,"w":"The arithmetic gives 14,700, but the line was only fitted between 40 and 120 m²."},{"q":"CAPM printout, n = 60: β = 1.35, SE 0.15. Test H0: β = 1 against β ≠ 1 at 5%.","o":["t = 9.00, reject","t = 2.33, df 58, row 40: 2.021, reject: riskier than the market","t = 2.33, do not reject","t = 0.35, do not reject","t = 1.35, do not reject"],"a":1,"w":"t = (1.35 − 1)/0.15 = 2.33. df = 58, not printed, so row 40: 2.021. Reject. The printed 9.00 tests β = 0, not 1."},{"q":"Same printout: α = 0.40, SE 0.50, p = 0.43. What about the manager's \"alpha\"?","o":["Significant at 5%","t = 0.80: no evidence of stock-picking skill","t = 0.80, reject at 10%","α must be 0 by definition","It proves the stock beats the market by 0.4% a month"],"a":1,"w":"t = 0.40/0.50 = 0.80, far below 2.021; p = 0.43 > 0.05 agrees. The alpha is a number, not a finding."}],"mistakes":["Writing the residual as ŷ − y.","Dividing the covariance by sₓ·sᵧ to get the slope. That gives r.","Interpreting an intercept or predicting at x values nobody observed.","Using the printed t (which tests 0) to judge β = 1.","Reading R² as \"right 75% of the time\". It is a share of variation."],"slides":[{"q":"x = 200, 250, 300, 350 and y = 180, 230, 280, 310. The least-squares line?","o":["ŷ = 250 + 0.88x","ŷ = 8 + 0.88x","ŷ = 8 + 1.14x","ŷ = 0.88 + 8x","ŷ = 275 + 0.88x"],"a":1,"w":"β̂₁ = (4·286,000 − 1,100·1,000)/(4·315,000 − 1,100²) = 44,000/50,000 = 0.88. β̂₀ = 250 − 0.88·275 = 8."},{"q":"Ads x = 1–5, sales y = 4, 7, 7, 10, 12 (₪100K). Sxy = 19, Sxx = 10, Σ(y − ȳ)² = 38. What is R²?","o":["0.975","0.95","0.50","1.9","0.19"],"a":1,"w":"r = 19/√(10·38) ≈ 0.975. R² = r² ≈ 0.95."},{"q":"Salary line ŷ = 7,657 + 494.6x. A 12-year employee earns 13,000. Residual?","o":["+592","About −592","13,592","−5,343","0"],"a":1,"w":"ŷ(12) ≈ 13,592. e = 13,000 − 13,592 ≈ −592: below the line."}]},{"n":9,"kind":"mock","title":"Mock 1","blurb":"The professor's practice exam 1, questions 1 to 18. Then the review, same day","src":"pe1"},{"n":10,"kind":"mock","title":"Mock 2","blurb":"The professor's practice exam 2, questions 1 to 18","src":"pe2"},{"n":11,"kind":"sweep","title":"The sweep","blurb":"Every topic, one task each, about five minutes each. Then your error log, top to bottom"},{"n":12,"kind":"mock","title":"Mock 3, the full practice run","blurb":"All 20 questions, including a difficult 19 and a very hard 20, as the real paper has. Sit it at the exam hour","src":"m3"},{"n":13,"kind":"warm","title":"Warm-up and the trap list","blurb":"Three familiar tasks and the mistakes this course punishes. Read it again an hour before. Nothing new"}];
const T=(h,r)=>'<table><tr><th>'+h[0]+'</th>'+h.slice(1).map(x=>'<td>'+x+'</td>').join('')+'</tr><tr><th>'+r[0]+'</th>'+r.slice(1).map(x=>'<td>'+x+'</td>').join('')+'</tr></table>';
const MOCKS = {
pe1:{label:'From practice exam 1',qs:[
{q:'In a customer survey, five variables were classified as shown. Exactly one classification is WRONG. Which one?',o:['Customer phone number: qualitative (nominal)','Highest degree earned (BA, MA, PhD): qualitative (ordinal)','Number of children in a household: quantitative, continuous','Country of birth: qualitative (nominal)','Weight of a parcel in kilograms: quantitative, continuous'],a:2,w:'Number of children is a count (0, 1, 2…), so it is discrete, not continuous.',l:'L1'},
{q:'A café owner asks the first two customers of the morning which milk they prefer. Both say oat milk, so he stops stocking regular milk. What is the main flaw?',o:['Voluntary-response (self-selection) bias','Survivorship bias','Small-sample fallacy: a conclusion from too few observations','Mistaking correlation for causation','Confirmation bias'],a:2,w:'Two observations cannot represent hundreds of customers. Confirmation bias would need him to ignore contrary evidence; he simply has almost none.',l:'L1'},
{q:'Defective items per shipment across 100 shipments. What percentage have fewer than 2 defective items?',t:T(['x','0','1','2','3','4'],['f','25','35','20','15','5']),o:['20%','60%','80%','40%','35%'],a:1,w:'F = 25, 60, 80, 95, 100. "Fewer than 2" = at most 1 = F(1) = 60, which is 60% of 100.',l:'L2'},
{q:'Waiting times of 60 patients, classes of UNEQUAL width. Which class is most crowded (highest density)?',t:T(['Minutes','0–20','20–40','40–50','50–55','55–75'],['f','10','14','12','9','15']),o:['0–20','20–40','40–50','50–55','55–75'],a:3,w:'d = 0.50, 0.70, 1.20, 1.80, 0.75. Highest is 50–55, even though 55–75 has the most cases.',l:'L2'},
{q:'A report shows nine competitors\' market shares as an exploded 3-D pie tilted toward the viewer; the front slice looks larger than a rear slice with the same percentage. Which statement is correct?',o:['The 3-D perspective distorts slice areas and nine categories are hard to compare by angle; a sorted bar chart would be more honest','It should show cumulative percentages instead','A pie is always best for parts of a whole, so it is fine','A histogram of the nine companies would fix it','The percentages cannot add up to 100% in 3-D'],a:0,w:'Perspective enlarges the front slice and nine slices defeat the eye. Competitors have no order (no cumulative), and histograms are for continuous data.',l:'L2'},
{q:'In a department of 40, one salary recorded as 50,000 is corrected to 500,000; it was already the highest. Which is correct?',o:['The mean rises; the median is unchanged','The mode rises','The median rises; the mean is unchanged','Neither changes','Both rise'],a:0,w:'The value stays last in the sorted list, so the median is untouched. The mean rises by 450,000/40 = 11,250.',l:'L3'},
{q:'A call centre handled 30 calls in the morning with mean 6 minutes and 20 in the afternoon with mean 11 minutes. Overall mean?',o:['11.0 minutes','8.5 minutes','13.3 minutes','7.5 minutes','8.0 minutes'],a:4,w:'(30·6 + 20·11)/50 = 400/50 = 8.0. 8.5 ignores the group sizes.',l:'L3'},
{q:'Two funds both average 8% a year over ten years; fund X has s = 12 points, fund Y s = 3. Which is correct?',o:['X beat Y every year','They cannot be compared, same mean','Y is riskier because it never reaches X\'s highs','X earned more on average','X\'s returns are far more dispersed around 8%; a cautious investor who cannot wait out a bad year should prefer Y'],a:4,w:'Same center, different spread. Smaller s is less risk.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Scores have mean 62 and s 8. The lecturer multiplies every score by 1.1, then adds 3. New mean and s?',o:['mean 71.2, s 8.8','mean 71.2, s 8.0','mean 68.2, s 8.8','mean 65.0, s 8.8','mean 71.2, s 11.8'],a:0,w:'Mean 1.1·62 + 3 = 71.2. s = 1.1·8 = 8.8; the added 3 never touches spread.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Rep X closed 120 deals (region mean 100, s 25); rep Y closed 60 (region mean 48, s 10). Who did better relative to their region?',o:['Equal, both above the mean','Rep X (z = +0.80 against +1.20)','Neither, different distributions','Rep Y (z = +1.20 against +0.80)','Rep X, 120 beats 60'],a:3,w:'X: (120 − 100)/25 = 0.80. Y: (60 − 48)/10 = 1.20.',l:'L5'},
{q:'A researcher wants to reduce the chance of a Type I error AND a Type II error at the same time. What achieves that?',o:['Switching to a one-sided test','Collecting a larger sample','Rounding t to one decimal','Raising α from 5% to 10%','Lowering α from 5% to 1%'],a:1,w:'With fixed n, α and β are a seesaw. Only more data shrinks the standard error and lowers both.',l:'L6'},
{q:'A firm suspects deliveries take LONGER than 3 days: H0: μ = 3, H1: μ > 3. t = 1.60, n = 25 (df 24). Which is correct?',o:['No verdict without x̄ and s','Reject at α = 0.01 two-sided','Reject at 0.05 two-sided, not at 0.01','Do not reject at 0.05 two-sided, but reject at 0.05 one-sided','Do not reject at α = 0.05, one-sided or two-sided'],a:4,w:'Row 24: one-tail 0.05 is 1.711, two-tail 0.05 is 2.064. 1.60 is below even 1.711.',l:'L6'},
{q:'A study finds r = 0 between customers\' age and monthly spending. Which is correct?',o:['Older customers spend exactly as much as younger ones','There is no linear association; a U-shaped relation is still possible','The regression slope is 1','The covariance must be positive','They are certainly unrelated'],a:1,w:'r = 0 only rules out a straight-line pattern. The slope and covariance would be 0.',l:'L6'},
{q:'A kiosk estimates ŷ = 40 − 2.5x (x = drink price in NIS, y = drinks sold per day). Which reading is correct?',o:['At price zero the model guarantees 40 drinks','Predicted sales at 10 NIS are 65','Raising price by one NIS will CAUSE sales to fall by 2.5','On average, each additional NIS of price is associated with 2.5 fewer drinks sold per day','250% of sales is caused by price'],a:3,w:'The slope is an average association, not a cause. The intercept is an anchor, not a guarantee. At 10 NIS the prediction is 15.',l:'L7'},
{q:'r = −0.9 between delivery distance and customer satisfaction. Which is correct?',o:['R² cannot be computed when r is negative','90% of the variation is explained by distance','R² = 0.9','R² = 0.81: 81% of the variation in satisfaction is explained by the linear relation, and the line slopes downward','R² = −0.81'],a:3,w:'R² = (−0.9)² = 0.81. The sign lives in r and the slope, never in R².',l:'L7'},
{q:'Branch A: n = 16, x̄ = 42, s = 8. Branch B: n = 16, x̄ = 38, s = 6. Test μ₁ = μ₂ against ≠ at 5%, df = n₁ + n₂ − 2. Which is correct?',o:['t ≈ 1.14, df 30: do not reject','t ≈ 1.60, df 30: do not reject','t ≈ 1.60, df 30: reject','t ≈ 1.60, df 15: do not reject','Cannot run, the s values differ'],a:1,w:'SE = √(64/16 + 36/16) = √6.25 = 2.5. t = 4/2.5 = 1.60. Row 30, two-tail 0.05: 2.042. 1.14 comes from adding the SEs instead of their squares.',l:'L6'},
{q:'House prices (thousand NIS), five-number summary: min 100, Q1 150, median 175, Q3 200, max 400. Which is correct?',o:['The median must equal the mean','Half the prices exceed 200','The IQR is 300','The whole distribution is symmetric because the box is','The middle half is symmetric (25 each side) and the IQR is 50; the maximum far beyond Q3 points to high outliers'],a:4,w:'IQR = 50 (300 is the range). A symmetric box says nothing about the tails.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Delivery times for 100 orders, values spread evenly within classes. Estimate the median.',t:T(['Days','0–2','2–4','4–5','5–8','8–12'],['f','12','28','25','30','5']),o:['4.1','4.5','4.4','4','5'],a:2,w:'F = 12, 40, 65. n/2 = 50 falls in 4–5 (F before 40, f 25). 4 + 10/25 × 1 = 4.4. 4.5 is the midpoint trap.',l:'L3'}]},
pe2:{label:'From practice exam 2',qs:[
{q:'Five variables were classified as shown. Exactly one classification is WRONG. Which one?',o:['Daily temperature in °C: quantitative, continuous','Number of cars per household: quantitative, discrete','Car brand: qualitative (nominal)','Hotel category, 1 to 5 stars: qualitative (ordinal)','National ID number: quantitative, discrete'],a:4,w:'An ID number is a label; its digits are not a quantity. It is nominal.',l:'L1'},
{q:'A news site invites visitors to vote: "Is this site the most reliable news source in the country?" 92% vote yes. The main flaw in treating 92% as public opinion?',o:['Voluntary-response (self-selection) bias','Survivorship bias','Small-sample fallacy','Correlation for causation','Confirmation bias'],a:0,w:'The voters chose themselves, and people who visit and vote are mostly fans. A big sample does not cure biased selection.',l:'L1'},
{q:'Support tickets per hour across 100 hours. What percentage of hours have more than 2 tickets?',t:T(['x','0','1','2','3','4'],['f','10','20','35','25','10']),o:['20%','65%','30%','35%','70%'],a:3,w:'F(2) = 65. More than 2 = n − F(2) = 35, which is 35%.',l:'L2'},
{q:'Order values of 80 orders, classes of UNEQUAL width (hundreds of NIS). Which class is most crowded?',t:T(['Class','1–5','5–10','10–20','20–30','30–32'],['f','8','15','24','26','7']),o:['1–5','5–10','10–20','20–30','30–32'],a:4,w:'d = 2.00, 3.00, 2.40, 2.60, 3.50. Highest is 30–32.',l:'L2'},
{q:'A manager sees a cumulative graph of customers served per hour rising all day and concludes "the number of customers grows every hour." Which is correct?',o:['A cumulative curve is a running total and can never fall; hourly growth is read from the slope, not the rise','The axis is truncated','Cumulative graphs are only for qualitative variables','It should be a pie chart','He is right'],a:0,w:'Even a quiet hour adds something, so F always rises. Growth would show as the curve getting steeper.',l:'L2'},
{q:'(i) A shoe retailer must choose ONE size to stock in bulk; (ii) a newspaper reports the "typical" house price in a city with a few enormous mansion sales. Which pair fits?',o:['(i) mode; (ii) mean','(i) median; (ii) mean','(i) mode; (ii) median','(i) mean; (ii) mode','(i) mean; (ii) mean'],a:2,w:'Stock the most common size. Mansions drag the mean; the median resists them.',l:'L3'},
{q:'60 units from machine X average 2.5 defects; 40 units from machine Y average 5.0. Overall mean?',o:['3.25','3.75','5.83','3.50','5.00'],a:3,w:'(60·2.5 + 40·5)/100 = 350/100 = 3.50.',l:'L3'},
{q:'A dataset is 30 identical values, all 7. Which is correct?',o:['Mean 7, median undefined','Range, IQR, variance and s are all zero, and mean = median = 7','Variance zero but s not','Range 7, IQR 0','s = 7'],a:1,w:'No variation, no spread.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Commute times: mean 34 minutes, s 9. Everyone adds a 10-minute walk, then times are expressed in hours. New mean and s?',o:['0.733 h, 0.150 h','0.733 h, 0.317 h','0.733 h, 9.000 h','0.567 h, 0.150 h','44 h, 0.150 h'],a:0,w:'Mean (34 + 10)/60 = 0.733. s = 9/60 = 0.150; the added 10 never touches spread.',l:'L4'},
{q:'A student scored 75 in Statistics (mean 65, s 5) and 85 in Marketing (mean 82, s 6). Where was the performance stronger?',o:['Marketing (z = 0.50 against 2.00)','Statistics (z = 2.00 against 0.50)','Marketing, 85 > 75','Cannot compare','Equal'],a:1,w:'Statistics (75 − 65)/5 = 2.00; Marketing (85 − 82)/6 = 0.50.',l:'L5'},
{q:'Airport security is designed with a deliberately LARGE α (many innocent passengers are stopped). Best explanation?',o:['A large α lowers both errors','A miss (Type II: an armed passenger passes) costs far more than a false alarm, so designers accept many false alarms to make β small','Searches are cheap, so Type I errors are impossible','They confused α with β','Large α is needed for small samples'],a:1,w:'Choosing α is pricing the two mistakes.',l:'L6'},
{q:'A school claims its graduates score ABOVE 500: H0: μ = 500, H1: μ > 500. t = 3.10, n = 13 (df 12). Which is correct?',o:['Do not reject at 0.05 either way','Do not reject two-sided at 0.05, reject one-sided','Reject at 0.05 two-sided, not at 0.01','No verdict without x̄ and s','Reject at α = 0.01, one-sided or two-sided'],a:4,w:'Row 12: two-tail 0.01 is 3.055. 3.10 clears it, so it clears every smaller bar too.',l:'L6'},
{q:'Across 30 summer weeks, ice-cream sales and drownings show r = 0.95. Which is correct?',o:['r cannot exceed 0.9 in real data','95% of drownings are caused by ice cream','Very strong co-movement, but hot weather is the plausible driver of both; no causal link is established','Ice cream causes drownings','A ban would cut drownings by 95%'],a:2,w:'A confounder moves both. r measures moving together, never cause.',l:'L6'},
{q:'ŷ = 2.3 + 1.9x (x = weekly ads, y = weekly sales, both thousand NIS). Which reading is correct?',o:['Zero ads guarantees 2.3 thousand in sales','Predicted sales at x = 5 are 9.5 thousand','On average, each extra thousand NIS of ads is associated with 1.9 thousand NIS more sales','Raising ads by a thousand will CAUSE sales to rise 1.9 thousand','190% of sales is caused by ads'],a:2,w:'Association, not cause. At x = 5 the prediction is 11.8.',l:'L7'},
{q:'Sales on advertising: R² = 0.25 and a POSITIVE slope. What is r?',o:['0.75','0.0625','0.25','−0.5','0.5'],a:4,w:'√0.25 = 0.5, positive like the slope. 0.75 is the unexplained share.',l:'L7'},
{q:'Sleep hours: treatment n = 13, x̄ = 7.7, s = 1.5; placebo n = 13, x̄ = 6.5, s = 1.2. Test equal means against ≠ at 5%, df = n₁ + n₂ − 2. Which is correct?',o:['t ≈ 2.25, df 12: reject at 5%','t ≈ 2.25, df 24: reject at 5% two-sided, not at 1%','Cannot run, the s values differ','t ≈ 2.25, df 24: do not reject at 5%','t ≈ 1.60, df 24: do not reject'],a:1,w:'SE = √(2.25/13 + 1.44/13) ≈ 0.53. t = 1.2/0.53 ≈ 2.25. Row 24: 2.064 at 5%, 2.797 at 1%.',l:'L6'},
{q:'Daily support tickets, five-number summary: min 2, Q1 5, median 6, Q3 12, max 13. Which is correct?',o:['The mean is 6','The IQR is 11','Half of the days had 5 to 6 tickets','The IQR is 7 and the upper half of the box is much wider: the middle 50% is right-skewed','25% of days had fewer than 2 tickets'],a:3,w:'IQR = 12 − 5 = 7 (11 is the range). The median is 1 above Q1 and 6 below Q3.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Monthly spending of 100 customers (hundreds of NIS), spread evenly within classes. Estimate the median.',t:T(['Class','0–10','10–20','20–30','30–50','50–100'],['f','15','25','30','20','10']),o:['20','25','21','23.33','30'],a:3,w:'F = 15, 40, 70. n/2 = 50 falls in 20–30 (F before 40, f 30). 20 + 10/30 × 10 = 23.33.',l:'L3'}]},
m3:{label:'Written for this plan',qs:[
{q:'Exactly one classification is WRONG. Which one?',o:['Employee ID: qualitative (nominal)','Years of experience: quantitative, continuous','Logins per day: quantitative, discrete','Customer tier (bronze, silver, gold): qualitative (ordinal)','Blood type (A, B, AB, O): quantitative, discrete'],a:4,w:'Blood types are unordered labels: nominal.',l:'L1'},
{q:'A gym advertises: "Members who train five times a week are the fittest in town, so our programme makes you fit." The main flaw?',o:['Small-sample fallacy','Survivorship bias','Leading question wording','Mistaking correlation for causation: fit people may simply choose to train more','Base-rate neglect'],a:3,w:'Training often and being fit move together, but fitness can also drive training. The direction of cause is not shown.',l:'L1'},
{q:'Cars per household in 100 households. What percentage have at least 2 cars?',t:T(['x','0','1','2','3','4'],['f','15','40','30','10','5']),o:['30%','45%','85%','55%','15%'],a:1,w:'F(1) = 55. At least 2 = n − F(1) = 45.',l:'L2'},
{q:'Order values of 80 orders (thousand NIS). Which class is most crowded?',t:T(['Class','0–5','5–10','10–12','12–20','20–40'],['f','10','20','12','24','14']),o:['0–5','5–10','10–12','12–20','20–40'],a:2,w:'d = 2, 4, 6, 3, 0.7. The narrow 10–12 class wins; 12–20 only has the most cases.',l:'L2'},
{q:'Twenty customers rated service Poor, Fair, Good or Excellent. Which presentation is best?',o:['A histogram with touching bars','A bar chart with the ratings in scale order, Poor to Excellent','A pie chart sorted by size','A line chart','A cumulative graph only'],a:1,w:'Ordinal data: bars keep the order a pie scrambles. A histogram needs a continuous scale.',l:'L2'},
{q:'In a class of 25, the lowest score is corrected from 30 to 3 (it stays the lowest). Which is correct?',o:['Mean and median both fall','The median falls, the mean does not','The mean falls, the median is unchanged','Neither changes','The mode falls'],a:2,w:'Order is unchanged, so the median is too. The mean drops by 27/25 = 1.08.',l:'L3'},
{q:'25 students averaged 70 and 75 students averaged 82. Overall mean?',o:['76','79','80.5','82','77'],a:1,w:'(25·70 + 75·82)/100 = (1,750 + 6,150)/100 = 79.',l:'L3'},
{q:'Two branches both average a 10-minute wait. Branch A has s = 1, branch B has s = 6. Which is correct?',o:['B has longer waits on average','A is riskier','Waits at B are far more spread out; a customer who cannot afford a long wait should pick A','They cannot be compared, same mean','B\'s waits are always longer than A\'s'],a:2,w:'Same center, different spread.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Temperatures have mean 20 °C and s 5 °C. Converted with F = 32 + 1.8·C, what are the new mean and s?',o:['68 °F, 9 °F','68 °F, 41 °F','36 °F, 9 °F','68 °F, 5 °F','20 °F, 9 °F'],a:0,w:'Mean 32 + 1.8·20 = 68. s = 1.8·5 = 9; the 32 does not touch spread.',l:'L4'},
{q:'Dana scored 540 on a test with mean 500 and s 100. Eli scored 27 on a test with mean 21 and s 5. Who did better relative to their group?',o:['Dana, 540 is much higher','Eli, z = 1.2 against 0.4','Dana, z = 1.2 against 0.4','Equal','Cannot compare'],a:1,w:'Dana (540 − 500)/100 = 0.4. Eli (27 − 21)/5 = 1.2.',l:'L5'},
{q:'With the sample size fixed, which change raises the chance of a Type II error?',o:['Raising α from 5% to 10%','Lowering α from 5% to 1%','Doubling the sample','Rounding t','Nothing changes β'],a:1,w:'A stricter α makes rejecting harder, so real effects get missed more often.',l:'L6'},
{q:'H0: μ = 50, H1: μ < 50. t = −2.10, n = 16. Which is correct?',o:['Reject at 5% one-sided (−1.753), but a two-sided 5% test (2.131) would not reject','Reject at 1% one-sided','Do not reject at 5% one-sided, t is negative','Reject at 5% two-sided','No verdict possible'],a:0,w:'df 15. One-tail 0.05: 1.753, so −2.10 is in the lower zone. One-tail 0.01 is 2.602: no. Two-tail 0.05 is 2.131: no.',l:'L6'},
{q:'Across 10 ordinary stores, r(ad spend, sales) = 0.15. Adding the flagship, with ten times the ads and sales of any other, makes r = 0.80. Which is correct?',o:['The true link is strong, report 0.80','One point manufactured the correlation; report 0.15 for comparable stores and treat the flagship separately','Delete the flagship as an error','Average the two: 0.475','r is unaffected by single points'],a:1,w:'r is built from means and standard deviations, and one wild point dominates both.',l:'L6'},
{q:'ŷ = 15 + 3x, where x is years of experience (data from 0 to 20) and y is salary in thousand NIS. Which reading is correct?',o:['Predicted salary at 10 years is 30','Each extra year CAUSES a 3,000 raise','On average, each extra year of experience is associated with 3 thousand NIS more salary','15 is the average salary','The intercept is meaningless because x = 0 is outside the data'],a:2,w:'At 10 years the prediction is 45. x = 0 is inside the data here, so 15 is a real starting-salary estimate.',l:'L7'},
{q:'A regression has R² = 0.49 and a NEGATIVE slope. What is r?',o:['0.49','−0.49','0.7','−0.7','−0.24'],a:3,w:'√0.49 = 0.7, with the slope\'s sign: −0.7.',l:'L7'},
{q:'Group 1: n = 20, x̄ = 55, s = 10. Group 2: n = 20, x̄ = 49, s = 8. Test equal means against ≠. Which is correct?',o:['t ≈ 1.49, df 38: do not reject','t ≈ 2.10, df 38, row 35: reject at 5% (2.030) but not at 1% (2.724)','t ≈ 2.10, df 19: do not reject at 5%','t ≈ 2.10, df 38: reject at 1%','t ≈ 0.60, do not reject'],a:1,w:'SE = √(100/20 + 64/20) = √8.2 ≈ 2.86. t = 6/2.86 ≈ 2.10. df 38 is not printed; nearest smaller row is 35. 1.49 comes from adding the SEs.',l:'L6'},
{q:'Five-number summary: min 10, Q1 30, median 35, Q3 40, max 42. Which is correct?',o:['IQR 32','The IQR is 10; the median sits closer to Q3 and the minimum lies far below Q1: a tail on the left','The distribution is right-skewed','Half the values are below 30','The mean must be above 35'],a:1,w:'IQR = 40 − 30 = 10. Everything points to a left tail, which usually pulls the mean below the median.',l:'L4'},
{q:'100 values, spread evenly within classes. Estimate the median.',t:T(['Class','0–4','4–6','6–10','10–20','20–30'],['f','20','30','25','15','10']),o:['5','6','5.5','8','6.4'],a:1,w:'F = 20, 50. n/2 = 50 is reached exactly at the end of 4–6: 4 + (50 − 20)/30 × 2 = 6. 5 is the midpoint trap.',l:'L3'},
{q:'Printout, n = 22: weekly sales on ad spend. Slope 1.8 (SE 0.75), intercept 12 (SE 5). A manager says each extra unit of ads brings more than one unit of sales. At 5%, two-sided, which is correct?',o:['The slope is not significant','The slope differs significantly from 0 (t = 2.4 > 2.086) but not from 1 (t ≈ 1.07); the data cannot support "more than one unit"','The slope differs significantly from 1','t = 2.4 so the slope is also significantly above 1','The intercept test settles it'],a:1,w:'df = 20. Against 0: 1.8/0.75 = 2.4, above 2.086. Against 1: 0.8/0.75 ≈ 1.07, well below. Testing 1 needs your own t.',l:'L7'},
{q:'One sample: x̄ = 53, s = 8 (divide by n), H0: μ = 50 against ≠, α = 5%. With n = 16 it does not reject. Keeping x̄ and s the same, what is the smallest n that rejects? (Use the table rows for n − 1.)',o:['25','28','29','30','36'],a:3,w:'t = 3/(8/√n). n = 29: t = 2.019 against 2.048 (row 28), no. n = 30: t = 2.054 against 2.045 (row 29), reject. Evidence is bought with data.',l:'L6'}]}
};
const TTABLE=[[1,3.078,6.314,12.706,31.821,63.657],[2,1.886,2.920,4.303,6.965,9.925],[3,1.638,2.353,3.182,4.541,5.841],[4,1.533,2.132,2.776,3.747,4.604],[5,1.476,2.015,2.571,3.365,4.032],[6,1.440,1.943,2.447,3.143,3.707],[7,1.415,1.895,2.365,2.998,3.499],[8,1.397,1.860,2.306,2.896,3.355],[9,1.383,1.833,2.262,2.821,3.250],[10,1.372,1.812,2.228,2.764,3.169],[11,1.363,1.796,2.201,2.718,3.106],[12,1.356,1.782,2.179,2.681,3.055],[13,1.350,1.771,2.160,2.650,3.012],[14,1.345,1.761,2.145,2.624,2.977],[15,1.341,1.753,2.131,2.602,2.947],[16,1.337,1.746,2.120,2.583,2.921],[17,1.333,1.740,2.110,2.567,2.898],[18,1.330,1.734,2.101,2.552,2.878],[19,1.328,1.729,2.093,2.539,2.861],[20,1.325,1.725,2.086,2.528,2.845],[21,1.323,1.721,2.080,2.518,2.831],[22,1.321,1.717,2.074,2.508,2.819],[23,1.319,1.714,2.069,2.500,2.807],[24,1.318,1.711,2.064,2.492,2.797],[25,1.316,1.708,2.060,2.485,2.787],[26,1.315,1.706,2.056,2.479,2.779],[27,1.314,1.703,2.052,2.473,2.771],[28,1.313,1.701,2.048,2.467,2.763],[29,1.311,1.699,2.045,2.462,2.756],[30,1.310,1.697,2.042,2.457,2.750],[35,1.306,1.690,2.030,2.438,2.724],[40,1.303,1.684,2.021,2.423,2.704],[60,1.296,1.671,2.000,2.390,2.660],[120,1.289,1.658,1.980,2.358,2.617],['∞ (z)',1.282,1.645,1.960,2.326,2.576]];
const TOPIC={L1:'Variables and biases',L2:'Tables and charts',L3:'Center',L4:'Spread',L5:'z-scores, CV, bell, Chebyshev',L6:'Hypothesis tests and correlation',L7:'Regression'};
const DAYTOPIC={1:'L1',2:'L2',3:'L3',4:'L4',5:'L5',6:'L6',7:'L6',8:'L7'};
const LET='abcde';
const dayFile=n=>'day-'+String(n).padStart(2,'0')+'.html';
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s);
const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"]/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;'}[c]));
const store={get(k,d){try{const v=localStorage.getItem(k);return v?JSON.parse(v):d}catch(e){return d}},set(k,v){try{localStorage.setItem(k,JSON.stringify(v))}catch(e){}}};
const K={err:'rm-errors-v1',done:'rm-done-v1',exam:'rm-exam-v1',mock:'rm-mock-v1'};

function rng(seed){let h=2166136261;for(const c of seed)h=Math.imul(h^c.charCodeAt(0),16777619);return()=>{h^=h<<13;h^=h>>>17;h^=h<<5;return((h>>>0)%10000)/10000}}
function order(id,len,shuffle){const idx=[...Array(len).keys()];if(!shuffle)return idx;const r=rng(id);for(let i=len-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(r()*(i+1));[idx[i],idx[j]]=[idx[j],idx[i]]}return idx}

function examDate(){const s=store.get(K.exam,null);return s?new Date(s+'T12:00:00'):null}
function dayDate(n){const e=examDate();if(!e)return null;const d=new Date(e);d.setDate(d.getDate()-(14-n));return d}
const fmt=d=>d.toLocaleDateString('en-GB',{weekday:'short',day:'numeric',month:'short'});
const fmtLong=d=>d.toLocaleDateString('en-GB',{weekday:'long',day:'numeric',month:'long',year:'numeric'});

function logError(e){const L=store.get(K.err,[]);const i=L.findIndex(x=>x.id===e.id);if(i>=0){L[i].misses=(L[i].misses||1)+1;L[i].chosen=e.chosen;L[i].at=Date.now()}else L.push({...e,misses:1,at:Date.now()});store.set(K.err,L)}

function qHTML(q,id,num,shuffle,mode){
  const ord=order(id,q.o.length,shuffle);
  return `<div class="q" data-id="${id}" data-mode="${mode}">
  <p class="qn">${num}</p><div class="qb"><p>${q.q}</p>${q.t?'<div class="tw">'+q.t+'</div>':''}
  <div class="opts">${ord.map((oi,k)=>`<button class="opt" data-oi="${oi}"><span class="lt">${LET[k]}</span><span>${esc(q.o[oi])}</span></button>`).join('')}</div>
  <div class="why" hidden></div></div></div>`}

let QREG={};
function reg(id,q,meta){QREG[id]={q,meta}}

function reveal(el,chosen){
  const {q}=QREG[el.dataset.id];
  el.querySelectorAll('.opt').forEach(b=>{b.disabled=true;const oi=+b.dataset.oi;if(oi===q.a)b.classList.add('right');else if(oi===chosen)b.classList.add('wrong')});
  const w=el.querySelector('.why');w.hidden=false;
  w.innerHTML=(chosen===q.a?'<b>Right.</b> ':chosen==null?'<b>Not answered.</b> ':'<b>Not this one.</b> ')+q.w;
}

function bindLearn(root){
  root.querySelectorAll('.q[data-mode=instant] .opt').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{
    const el=b.closest('.q'),oi=+b.dataset.oi,{q,meta}=QREG[el.dataset.id];
    reveal(el,oi);
    if(oi!==q.a)logError({id:el.dataset.id,src:meta.src,topic:meta.topic,q:q.q.replace(/<[^>]+>/g,''),chosen:q.o[oi],correct:q.o[q.a]});
  });
  root.querySelectorAll('.q[data-mode=exam] .opt').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{
    const el=b.closest('.q');if(el.classList.contains('marked'))return;
    el.querySelectorAll('.opt').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('picked'));b.classList.add('picked');updateCount();
  });
}

function doneToggle(n){const D=store.get(K.done,{});D[n]=!D[n];store.set(K.done,D);render()}

function strip(){
  const D=store.get(K.done,{});
  return `<div class="strip" aria-label="Progress">${DAYS.map(d=>`<a href="${dayFile(d.n)}" class="dot ${d.kind} ${D[d.n]?'on':''}" title="Day ${d.n}: ${esc(d.title)}"><span>${d.n}</span></a>`).join('')}<span class="dot flag" title="Exam">Exam</span></div>`}

function dayRow(d){
  const D=store.get(K.done,{}),dt=dayDate(d.n);
  const tag={learn:'Learn',mock:'Timed',sweep:'Review',warm:'Calm'}[d.kind];
  return `<a class="row ${D[d.n]?'done':''}" href="${dayFile(d.n)}"><span class="rn">${d.n}</span><span class="rt"><b>${esc(d.title)}</b><span class="meta">${tag}${dt?' · '+fmt(dt):''}${D[d.n]?' · done':''}</span><span class="rb">${esc(d.blurb)}</span></span></a>`}

function errorsBlock(){
  const L=store.get(K.err,[]).sort((a,b)=>(b.misses||1)-(a.misses||1));
  if(!L.length)return `<p class="empty">Nothing yet. Every question you get wrong lands here, in this browser, so you can come back to it.</p>`;
  const by={};L.forEach(e=>{by[e.topic]=(by[e.topic]||0)+1});
  return `<p class="muted">${L.length} question${L.length>1?'s':''} missed. By topic: ${Object.entries(by).sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).map(([t,c])=>esc(TOPIC[t]||t)+' '+c).join(', ')}.</p>
  <ol class="elog">${L.map(e=>`<li><p>${esc(e.q)}</p><p class="muted">You chose: ${esc(e.chosen)}<br>Right answer: ${esc(e.correct)}${e.misses>1?'<br>Missed '+e.misses+' times':''}</p></li>`).join('')}</ol>
  <div class="btns"><button class="btn" id="copyPrompt">Copy a prompt for Claude</button><button class="btn ghost" id="clearLog">Clear the log</button></div><p class="muted small" id="copyMsg"></p>`}

function prompt(){
  const L=store.get(K.err,[]);
  return `I'm studying for the Research Methods final (Bar-Ilan IMBA, course 70-348, Prof. Yevgeny Mugerman). The exam is 20 multiple-choice questions with five options (a) to (e); a t-table is attached.
Course conventions: variance and standard deviation divide by n; quartile positions use (n + 1); two-sample t uses df = n1 + n2 - 2 and SE = sqrt(s1^2/n1 + s2^2/n2); if a df is not in the table, use the nearest smaller row; one-sided claims read the one-tail header, "differs" reads the two-tail header.

These are the questions I got wrong:
${L.map((e,i)=>`${i+1}. [${TOPIC[e.topic]||e.topic}] ${e.q}
   I chose: ${e.chosen}
   Correct: ${e.correct}`).join('\n')}

Please find the pattern in my mistakes and explain it in plain English in a few sentences. Then test me: write new exam-style multiple-choice questions (five options) on exactly these weak spots, one at a time. Wait for my answer before giving the solution.`}

function bindErrors(){
  const c=$('#copyPrompt');if(c)c.onclick=async()=>{const t=prompt();try{await navigator.clipboard.writeText(t);$('#copyMsg').textContent='Copied. Paste it into a new Claude chat.'}catch(e){const ta=document.createElement('textarea');ta.value=t;ta.className='pt';c.parentNode.after(ta);ta.select();$('#copyMsg').textContent='Your browser blocked copying. Select the text below and copy it.'}};
  const x=$('#clearLog');if(x)x.onclick=()=>{if(confirm('Clear every saved mistake? This cannot be undone.')){store.set(K.err,[]);render()}};
}

function home(){
  const e=examDate(),D=store.get(K.done,{}),nd=Object.values(D).filter(Boolean).length;
  const val=store.get(K.exam,'');
  return `<header class="hero">
  <p class="kicker">Research Methods, Prof. Yevgeny Mugerman</p>
  <h1>13 days to the final</h1>
  <p class="lede">Eight days to learn it, three papers to test it, one day to catch what you missed, one to warm up.</p>
  ${strip()}
  <div class="exam"><label for="ex">Exam date</label><input type="date" id="ex" value="${val}"><span class="muted">${e?'Exam: '+fmtLong(e)+'. Every day below now has its date.':'Set it and each day gets a date, counted back from the exam.'} ${nd?nd+' of 13 days done.':''}</span></div>
  </header>
  <nav class="tools"><a href="formulas.html" class="tool"><b>Every formula, on one page</b><span>Grouped by lesson, each with a line on when it applies, plus what makes it go wrong. Keep it open while you work.</span></a><a href="t-table.html" class="tool"><b>The t-table</b><span>The same rows as the table attached to the exam, with how to read it.</span></a></nav>
  <section><h2>How to use this</h2>
  <p>A learn day takes about an hour: one question looking back at an older topic, fifteen minutes on the idea, thirty on six questions plus the ones taken from the lecture slides, five reading the common mistakes. A mock day takes about three hours, because it is the paper plus going through what you missed.</p>
  <p>Every question you get wrong is saved to your <b>error log</b>, in this browser. On the sweep day, and at the bottom of each mock, a button turns that log into a prompt you can paste into Claude, so it tests you on exactly what you keep missing.</p>
  <p class="muted">The log lives only in this browser, on this device. Clearing your browsing data erases it. The prompt text is also your backup: copy it out and it is safe anywhere.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Learn</h2><div class="rows">${DAYS.filter(d=>d.n<=8).map(dayRow).join('')}</div></section>
  <section><h2>Test</h2><div class="rows">${DAYS.filter(d=>d.n>=9&&d.n<=12).map(dayRow).join('')}</div></section>
  <section><h2>Warm up</h2><div class="rows">${DAYS.filter(d=>d.n===13).map(dayRow).join('')}</div></section>
  <section id="log"><h2>Your error log</h2>${errorsBlock()}</section>
  <section><h2>The paper itself</h2>
  <dl class="facts"><div><dt>Length</dt><dd>Announced on Moodle</dd></div><div><dt>Questions</dt><dd>20, all multiple choice</dd></div><div><dt>Marks</dt><dd>5 points each</dd></div><div><dt>Materials</dt><dd>Printed or handwritten notes, open</dd></div><div><dt>Calculator</dt><dd>Allowed</dd></div><div><dt>Whole plan</dt><dd>About 19 hours</dd></div></dl>
  <p>No phones, laptops, tablets, smart watches, or anything that can communicate or store files. The duration is announced on Moodle. Because your notes are allowed in, print the formula page and the trap list from day 13.</p>
  <p>The practice papers follow a fixed pattern: questions 1 to 13 easy, 14 to 18 intermediate, 19 difficult, 20 very hard. They check units, the direction of the test, and the course conventions: divide by n, and two-sample df = n₁ + n₂ − 2.</p>
  <p class="muted">The final is worth 80% of the course grade, next to homework (10%), participation (5%) and quizzes (5%), as the Session 3 exercises put it.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Where this came from, and what it is not</h2>
  <p>Everything here is built from your course files: the slides for all seven lessons, the class exercises for sessions 1 to 3, class assignments 4, 5, 6A, 6B and 7, and the two practice exams with their solutions.</p>
  <p><b>Mocks 1 and 2 are the professor's own practice papers</b>, questions 1 to 18. Questions 19 and 20 were not in the files you shared, so those papers stop at 18. Mock 3 and the six questions on each learn day were written for this plan. The "From the slides" questions are the professor's own worked examples and true/false traps, turned into multiple choice. None of it is a real past final.</p><p>Where two files disagree, the page says so. The one that matters: two-sample degrees of freedom. See day 6.</p>
  <p>Every number in every new question was worked out again in code before it went in.</p></section>`}

function learnDay(d){
  const t=DAYTOPIC[d.n];let h='',num=1;
  if(d.review){const id=`d${d.n}r`;reg(id,d.review,{src:'Day '+d.n,topic:DAYTOPIC[d.n-1]||t});h+=`<section><h2>Look back</h2><p class="muted">Five minutes on an older topic, so it stays fresh.</p>${qHTML(d.review,id,'↺',true,'instant')}</section>`}
  h+=`<section class="idea"><h2>The idea</h2>${d.idea}</section>`;
  h+=`<section><h2>Six questions</h2><p class="muted">Pick an answer and the explanation opens. Wrong answers go to your error log.</p>${d.qs.map((q,i)=>{const id=`d${d.n}q${i}`;reg(id,q,{src:'Day '+d.n,topic:t});return qHTML(q,id,i+1,true,'instant')}).join('')}</section>`;
  if(d.slides){h+=`<section><h2>From the slides</h2><p class="muted">Worked examples and exam traps from the Lesson ${d.n<=5?d.n:d.n===8?7:6} slides, turned into questions.</p>${d.slides.map((q,i)=>{const id=`d${d.n}s${i}`;reg(id,q,{src:'Day '+d.n+' slides',topic:t});return qHTML(q,id,i+7,true,'instant')}).join('')}</section>`}
  h+=`<section><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul class="traps">${d.mistakes.map(m=>`<li>${m}</li>`).join('')}</ul></section>`;
  return h}

let timer=null,t0=null;
function mockDay(d){
  const M=MOCKS[d.src],shuffle=d.src==='m3',res=store.get(K.mock,{})[d.src];
  const n=M.qs.length;
  let intro={pe1:'The professor\'s practice exam 1. The real paper has 20 questions; the file you shared had 18, so this runs 18. Work it as if it counts, with your notes and the t-table beside you.',pe2:'The professor\'s practice exam 2, questions 1 to 18. It mirrors the pattern of paper 1 with new numbers, so a question that caught you on Mock 1 should not catch you twice.',m3:'The full practice run: all 20 questions, with the same easy, intermediate, difficult and very hard pattern as the real paper. Sit it at the same hour as the exam. A lower score than Mock 1 is normal.'}[d.src];
  let h=`<section><p>${intro}</p>${res?`<p class="muted">Last time: ${res.score} of ${res.n}${res.min?' in '+res.min+' minutes':''}.</p>`:''}
  <div class="clock" id="clock"><button class="btn" id="startT">Start the clock</button><span id="tval" class="tval">0:00</span><span class="muted" id="cnt">0 of ${n} answered</span></div></section>
  <section>${M.qs.map((q,i)=>{const id=`${d.src}q${i}`;reg(id,q,{src:M.label,topic:q.l});return `<p class="tag">${M.label}${d.src!=='m3'?', question '+(i+1):''}</p>`+qHTML(q,id,i+1,shuffle,'exam')}).join('')}
  <div class="btns"><button class="btn" id="markIt">Mark my paper</button></div><div id="score"></div></section>`;
  return h}
function updateCount(){const c=$('#cnt');if(!c)return;const tot=document.querySelectorAll('.q[data-mode=exam]').length,a=document.querySelectorAll('.q[data-mode=exam] .opt.picked').length;c.textContent=`${a} of ${tot} answered`}
function bindMock(d){
  const s=$('#startT');if(s)s.onclick=()=>{t0=Date.now();s.disabled=true;s.textContent='Clock running';clearInterval(timer);timer=setInterval(()=>{const m=Math.floor((Date.now()-t0)/60000),sec=Math.floor((Date.now()-t0)/1000)%60;$('#tval').textContent=`${m}:${String(sec).padStart(2,'0')}`},1000)};
  $('#markIt').onclick=()=>{
    const qs=[...document.querySelectorAll('.q[data-mode=exam]')];
    const blank=qs.filter(el=>!el.querySelector('.opt.picked')).length;
    if(blank&&!confirm(blank+' question'+(blank>1?'s are':' is')+' still blank. Blanks count as wrong but stay out of your error log. Mark the paper now?'))return;let sc=0;const missed={};
    qs.forEach(el=>{const p=el.querySelector('.opt.picked'),ch=p?+p.dataset.oi:null,{q,meta}=QREG[el.dataset.id];el.classList.add('marked');reveal(el,ch);
      if(ch===q.a)sc++;else if(ch==null){missed[q.l]=(missed[q.l]||0)+1}else{missed[q.l]=(missed[q.l]||0)+1;logError({id:el.dataset.id,src:meta.src,topic:q.l,q:q.q.replace(/<[^>]+>/g,''),chosen:q.o[ch],correct:q.o[q.a]})}});
    clearInterval(timer);const min=t0?Math.round((Date.now()-t0)/60000):null;
    const R=store.get(K.mock,{});R[d.src]={score:sc,n:qs.length,min};store.set(K.mock,R);
    $('#markIt').disabled=true;
    $('#score').innerHTML=`<div class="result"><p class="big">${sc} of ${qs.length}</p><p>That is ${Math.round(sc/qs.length*100)} out of 100${min!=null?', in '+min+' minutes':''}.</p>${Object.keys(missed).length?`<p>Missed by topic: ${Object.entries(missed).sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).map(([t,c])=>esc(TOPIC[t])+' '+c).join(', ')}. Scroll up: every question now shows its explanation.</p>`:'<p>Clean paper.</p>'}</div><h2>Your error log</h2>${errorsBlock()}`;
    bindErrors();$('#score').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});
  };
}

const SWEEP=[
['Variables','Hotel stars coded 1 to 5. Type, and which centers are legal?','Qualitative, ordinal. Mode and median; the mean assumes equal gaps the scale does not promise.'],
['Tables','x = 0, 1, 2, 3 with f = 12, 18, 14, 6. How many values are at least 2?','n = 50, F(1) = 30, so 50 − 30 = 20.'],
['Center','30 people averaged 40, and 10 people averaged 80. Overall mean?','(30·40 + 10·80)/40 = 2,000/40 = 50. Not 60.'],
['Spread','Mean 50, s 10. Every value is doubled and 5 is added. New mean, s and variance?','Mean 105, s 20, variance 400. The 5 touches only the mean.'],
['Relative position','Mean 70, s 8. Which score has z = +1.25? At least what share lies within 2 s, shape unknown?','x = 70 + 1.25·8 = 80. Chebyshev: at least 75%.'],
['Hypothesis tests','n = 16, x̄ = 104, s = 8, H1: μ > 100, α = 5%. Verdict?','SE = 8/4 = 2, t = 2, df 15. One-tail 0.05 = 1.753. Reject: enough evidence that μ > 100.'],
['Correlation','cov(x, y) = −12, sₓ = 4, sᵧ = 5. What is r, and where on the ladder?','r = −12/20 = −0.6: medium, negative.'],
['Regression','x̄ = 4, ȳ = 20, slope 3. Intercept? Predicted y at x = 6? If r = 0.9, what is R²?','β̂₀ = 20 − 3·4 = 8. ŷ(6) = 26. R² = 0.81.']];
const WARM=[
['Read the table','Two samples of 14 and 14, "the means differ", α = 5%. Critical value?','df = 26, two-tail 0.05: 2.056.'],
['Grouped median','Classes 0–10: 20, 10–20: 40, 20–40: 40 (n = 100). Median?','F = 20, 60. n/2 = 50 falls in 10–20: 10 + (50 − 20)/40 × 10 = 17.5.'],
['From R² to r','R² = 0.36, the line slopes down. r?','−0.6.']];
const TRAPS=['Digits used as labels (IDs, phone numbers, shirt numbers) are nominal.','Ratings 1 to 5 are ordinal. Their mean is a risky shortcut.','"At least x" is n minus F of the value one step below x.','Uneven class widths: rank crowding by d = f/ℓ, never by f.','Grouped median: walk into the class. The midpoint is the trap answer.','Combined mean: weight each group by its size.','Divide by n. Always. Quartile positions use n + 1.','Adding a constant never changes s. Multiplying by b scales s by |b| and the variance by b².','Standardize each person against their own group.','No CV when the mean is near zero, or for °C and rating scales.','68-95-99.7 needs a bell shape. Otherwise it is Chebyshev: at least 1 − 1/k².','One direction in H1 reads the one-tail header; "differs" reads the two-tail header.','The denominator is s/√n. Two samples: add the squares under one root.','Two-sample df = n₁ + n₂ − 2. df not printed: nearest smaller row.','Lower-tail test: reject only below −t*.','Never write "accept H0". Write "not enough evidence to reject".','Only a bigger sample lowers both α and β.','r = 0 means no straight-line link, not no link. One outlier can make or break r.','R² is never negative. r = ±√R², with the slope\'s sign.','Residual = y − ŷ.','The printed t tests "= 0". For β = 1, compute (β̂ − 1)/SE yourself.','No predictions outside the data range. A slope is association, not cause.','Right skew: more than half sit below the mean. Left skew: more than half sit above it.','Fifth decile = median. In a left-skewed set its z is positive.','A raise or bonus for everyone never moves anyone\'s z.','Range 0 forces IQR 0. IQR 0 forces nothing about the range.','Σx² is not (Σx)². Σxy is not Σx·Σy.','Grouped mode: the midpoint of the highest-density class, not the highest f.','If a question states its own df rule, follow the question. Otherwise two-sample df = n₁ + n₂ − 2.'];
function tasks(list){return list.map(([t,q,a],i)=>`<div class="task"><p class="qn">${i+1}</p><div><p><b>${t}.</b> ${q}</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>${a}</p></details></div></div>`).join('')}
function sweepDay(){return `<section><p>One task per lesson, about five minutes each. Do it on paper first, then open the answer. If one wobbles, reopen that learn day.</p>${tasks(SWEEP)}</section><section id="log"><h2>Your error log, top to bottom</h2><p class="muted">Most-missed first. For each one, say out loud why the right answer is right before moving on. Then copy the prompt and let Claude test you on the pattern.</p>${errorsBlock()}</section>`}
function warmDay(){return `<section><p>Three familiar tasks to get your hands moving. Nothing new today.</p>${tasks(WARM)}</section><section><h2>The trap list</h2><p class="muted">Every item is a mistake the course materials call out. Print it and bring it; notes are allowed. Read it once more an hour before.</p><ul class="traps">${TRAPS.map(t=>`<li>${t}</li>`).join('')}</ul></section>`}

function dayPage(n){
  const d=DAYS.find(x=>x.n===n);if(!d)return home();
  const dt=dayDate(n),D=store.get(K.done,{});
  const tag={learn:'Learn',mock:'Timed',sweep:'Review',warm:'Calm'}[d.kind];
  let body=d.kind==='learn'?learnDay(d):d.kind==='mock'?mockDay(d):d.kind==='sweep'?sweepDay():warmDay();
  const prev=DAYS.find(x=>x.n===n-1),next=DAYS.find(x=>x.n===n+1);
  return `<p class="back"><a href="index.html">All 13 days</a></p><header class="dh"><p class="kicker">Day ${n} · ${tag}${dt?' · '+fmt(dt):''}</p><h1>${esc(d.title)}</h1><p class="lede">${esc(d.blurb)}</p></header>${body}
  <div class="endbar"><button class="btn ${D[n]?'ghost':''}" id="doneBtn">${D[n]?'Marked done. Undo':'Mark day '+n+' done'}</button>
  <span class="pn">${prev?`<a href="${dayFile(prev.n)}">Day ${prev.n}</a>`:''}${next?`<a href="${dayFile(next.n)}">Day ${next.n}</a>`:''}</span></div>`}

const FORM=[
['Lesson 1 · Variables',[['Nominal → mode. Ordinal → mode, median. Quantitative → mode, median, mean.','Which centers a type allows.'],['Parameter = population truth. Statistic = its sample estimate.','Anything computed from the sample is a statistic.']]],
['Lesson 2 · Tables and charts',[['p(x) = f(x)/n','Relative frequency; comparable across samples.'],['At most x = F(x). At least x = n − F(one step below x).','Discrete or ordered values only.'],['ℓ = upper − lower; d = f/ℓ; d% = p/ℓ','Grouped data. Histogram heights are d; area = f; total area = n.']]],
['Lesson 3 · Center',[['Σa = n·a; Σa·x = a·Σx; Σ(x + y) = Σx + Σy; but Σxy ≠ Σx·Σy and Σx² ≠ (Σx)²','Expand first, then sum term by term.'],['Grouped mode = midpoint of the class with the highest density','Grouped data.'],['Harmonic mean = n / Σ(1/x); geometric mean = (x₁·x₂·…)^(1/n)','Average speed over equal distances; compounding growth.'],['Median at position (n + 1)/2 of the sorted data','Even n: average the two middle values.'],['Mean from a table = Σx·f / n','Discrete table.'],['Combined mean = (n₁x̄₁ + n₂x̄₂)/(n₁ + n₂)','Groups of different sizes.'],['Grouped median = L + ((n/2 − F before)/f) × width','Class where F first reaches n/2.'],['Mean > median → right tail; mean < median → left tail','Reading skew.']]],
['Lesson 4 · Spread',[['Q1 at (n + 1)/4, Q3 at 3(n + 1)/4; IQR = Q3 − Q1; range = max − min','Sorted raw data.'],['s² = Σ(x − x̄)²/n = Σx²/n − x̄²; s = √s²','Divide by n, always.'],['Grouped: mean = Σf·m/n, s² = Σf·m²/n − x̄² (m = midpoint)','Grouped data.'],['Percentile P = L + ((p·n − F before)/f) × width','Grouped data.'],['Dial = n·z/100; walk F; first value whose F reaches the dial is Pz','Frequency tables (the slides\' method).'],['Symmetric if Q3 − Q2 = Q2 − Q1; upper gap bigger → right skew','Skew from quartiles; outlier-proof.'],['y = a + b·x → ȳ = a + b·x̄, s_y = |b|·s_x, s²_y = b²·s²_x','Adding or scaling every value.']]],
['Lesson 5 · Relative position',[['z = (x − x̄)/s; x = x̄ + z·s','Compare across scales; each against its own group.'],['CV = s/x̄','Spread per unit of mean. Not for means near 0 or arbitrary zeros.'],['Bell: 68% ±1s, 95% ±2s (1.960), 99.7% ±3s, 90% ±1.645s; top 5% above x̄ + 1.645s','Only if the shape is shown to be bell-like.'],['Chebyshev: at least 1 − 1/k² within k·s; at most 1/k² beyond','Any shape. k = 2 → 75% in, 25% out; k = 3 → 88.9% in, 11% out.'],['|z| < 1 crowd · 1–2 notable · 2–3 rare · > 3 alarm','Reading how extreme a value is.']]],
['Lesson 6A · Hypothesis tests',[['One sample: t = (x̄ − μ₀)/(s/√n), df = n − 1','One mean against a claimed value.'],['Two samples: t = (x̄₁ − x̄₂)/√(s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂), df = n₁ + n₂ − 2','Two independent groups.'],['Two-sided: reject if |t| > t*. One-sided: reject if t is past t* in H1\'s direction','Top header one-tail; bottom header two-tail.'],['Type I = reject a true H0 (chance α). Type II = miss a false H0 (β). Power = 1 − β','Lower α → higher β; bigger n lowers both.'],['Reject if p < α','Software output instead of the table.']]],
['Lesson 6B · Correlation',[['cov = Σdₓ·dᵧ/n','Sign only; size has mixed units.'],['r = cov/(sₓ·sᵧ), −1 to +1','Ladder: 0.10 weak, 0.40 medium, 0.70 strong, 0.90 very strong.'],['var = w²·var(x) + (1 − w)²·var(y) + 2w(1 − w)·cov','Two-asset portfolio.']]],
['Lesson 7 · Regression',[['β̂₁ = Σdₓdᵧ/Σdₓ² = (nΣxy − ΣxΣy)/(nΣx² − (Σx)²); β̂₀ = ȳ − β̂₁·x̄','Least squares; line passes through (x̄, ȳ).'],['β̂₁ = cov/sₓ² = r·sᵧ/sₓ','Slope and r always share a sign.'],['e = y − ŷ; Σe = 0','Data minus model.'],['R² = 1 − Σe²/Σdᵧ² = r²','Share of variation explained.'],['t = (coefficient − tested value)/SE, df = n − 2','Printouts. The printed t tests 0.'],['CAPM: (R − r_f) = α + β(R_m − r_f)','β > 1 aggressive, β < 1 defensive.']]]];
function formulas(){return `<p class="back"><a href="index.html">All 13 days</a></p><header class="dh"><h1>Every formula, on one page</h1><p class="lede">Nothing here has to be memorised; the exam is open-materials. What you practise is knowing which one applies, and why. Print this page and bring it.</p></header>
${FORM.map(([h,rows])=>`<section class="fs"><h2>${h}</h2>${rows.map(([f,w])=>`<div class="fr"><div class="f">${f}</div><p class="muted">${w}</p></div>`).join('')}</section>`).join('')}
<section><h2>Things that make a formula wrong</h2><ul class="traps"><li>Dividing by n − 1 instead of n.</li><li>Forgetting √n in the standard error, or adding standard errors instead of squares.</li><li>Reading the wrong header of the t-table for the direction of H1.</li><li>Using heights f instead of d when class widths differ.</li><li>Averaging group means without weighting by size.</li><li>Adding a constant to s, or not squaring the factor for the variance.</li></ul></section>`}
function table(){return `<p class="back"><a href="index.html">All 13 days</a></p><header class="dh"><h1>The t-table</h1><p class="lede">The same rows as the table attached to the practice exams.</p></header>
<section><p>Row = degrees of freedom: n − 1 for one sample, n₁ + n₂ − 2 for two, n − 2 for a regression printout. A claim with a direction (&gt; or &lt;) reads the top header. "Differs" (≠) reads the bottom header. If your df is not printed, use the nearest smaller row. The last row is z.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tt"><thead><tr><th>df</th><th>one-tail 0.10<br><span>two-tail 0.20</span></th><th>0.05<br><span>0.10</span></th><th>0.025<br><span>0.05</span></th><th>0.01<br><span>0.02</span></th><th>0.005<br><span>0.01</span></th></tr></thead><tbody>${TTABLE.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th>${r.slice(1).map(v=>`<td>${v.toFixed(3)}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div></section>`}

function render(){
  QREG={};clearInterval(timer);t0=null;
  const pg=document.body.dataset.page||'home';let html,day=null;
  if(pg.startsWith('day-')){day=+pg.slice(4);html=dayPage(day);const d=DAYS.find(x=>x.n===day);document.title='Day '+day+': '+(d?d.title:'')+' · Research Methods'}
  else if(pg==='formulas'){html=formulas();document.title='Every formula · Research Methods'}
  else if(pg==='table'){html=table();document.title='The t-table · Research Methods'}
  else{html=home();document.title='Research Methods: 13 days to the final'}
  document.getElementById('app').innerHTML=html;
  bindLearn(document.getElementById('app'));bindErrors();
  const ex=$('#ex');if(ex)ex.onchange=()=>{store.set(K.exam,ex.value||null);render()};
  const db=$('#doneBtn');if(db)db.onclick=()=>doneToggle(day);
  if(day){const d=DAYS.find(x=>x.n===day);if(d&&d.kind==='mock')bindMock(d)}
}
render();
